{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0186v4LbUyc9HcCT8qVprHrZ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\appendix\n2\t\n3\t\\section{Direction Asymmetry and Quality Confounding}\n4\t\\label{app:direction}\n5\t\n6\tThe formality axis exhibits a pronounced direction asymmetry: the judge selects the formal variant in 78.8\\% of formal-is-rewrite pairs ($p = 0.001$) but only 54.5\\% of casual-is-rewrite pairs ($p = 0.83$).\n7\tThis asymmetry suggests that original quality $Q$ and formality $S$ are partially confounded---formally written texts may be inherently perceived as higher quality.\n8\tTo quantify this, we fit a GEE logistic model (exchangeable correlation) with both formality and original-preference as predictors.\n9\tAfter controlling for original preference, the formality effect remains significant ($\\text{OR} = 2.58$, $p = 0.002$), and the original-preference effect is also significant ($\\text{OR} = 2.32$, $p = 0.006$).\n10\tThe formality preference thus survives adjustment for original-preference confounding, though the significant original-preference effect indicates that quality--style confounding is present and non-negligible.\n11\t\n12\tAt $n = 88$, the Qwen3-32B formality Style OR ($4.20$) exceeded the GPT-4o estimate ($2.58$), but a scaled replication ($n = 355$) corrects this to $2.15$ ($p < 0.0001$), comparable to GPT-4o and consistent with small-sample upward bias rather than evaluator-producer inflation of the style effect.\n13\tThe originality confound remains stronger ($4.99$ vs.\\ $2.32$), consistent with evaluator-producer bias inflating the originality component (Limitations).\n14\tThe directional agreement between judges---both favor formal text after controlling for originality---supports the existence of formality bias, but the effect magnitude from Qwen3-32B should not be directly compared with GPT-4o.\n15\t\n16\tThe NLI gate blocks \\emph{content} changes but cannot block quality perception shifts that are themselves mediated by style---a fundamental identifiability limit of any text-rewriting approach.\n17\t\n18\t\\section{Consistency Filter Robustness}\n19\t\\label{sec:consistency}\n20\t\n21\tThe position-consistency filter retains 55 of 98 valid pairs (56.1\\%); consistent and inconsistent groups show no significant differences across four covariates (NLI score $p = 0.824$, BERTScore $p = 0.12$, text length $p = 0.137$, length diff $p = 0.715$), indicating selection on judge decisiveness rather than text properties.\n22\tThe BT model using all 173 comparisons including inconsistent pairs yields 63.0\\% ($p = 0.0004$), consistent with the filtered sign test (73.9\\%, $p = 0.006$), confirming no systematic bias from the filter.\n23\t\n24\t\\section{GEE Model Diagnostics}\n25\t\\label{app:gee_diagnostics}\n26\t\n27\t\\paragraph{Correlation structure robustness.}\n28\tTable~\\ref{tab:gee_corr} compares exchangeable and independence working correlation structures across all six judge$\\times$axis cells.\n29\tThe two structures yield virtually identical results: $|\\Delta\\text{QIC}| < 0.001$ and maximum coefficient change ${<}0.02$ in every cell, with estimated within-pair correlations $\\hat{\\alpha} \\in [-0.25, +0.11]$.\n30\tAll GEE results reported in the main text are therefore robust to correlation structure specification.\n31\t\n32\t\\begin{table}[h]\n33\t\\centering\n34\t\\small\n35\t\\caption{GEE correlation structure comparison across six judge$\\times$axis cells.}\n36\t\\label{tab:gee_corr}\n37\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n38\t\\toprule\n39\tConfig & Exch.\\ QIC & Indep.\\ QIC & $|\\Delta\\text{QIC}|$ & Max$|\\Delta\\text{Coef}|$ \\\\\n40\t\\midrule\n41\tGPT-4o form.\\ & --- & --- & ${<}0.001$ & ${<}0.02$ \\\\\n42\tQwen3 form.\\ & --- & --- & ${<}0.001$ & ${<}0.02$ \\\\\n43\tGPT-4o verb.\\ & --- & --- & ${<}0.001$ & ${<}0.02$ \\\\\n44\tQwen3 verb.\\ & --- & --- & ${<}0.001$ & ${<}0.02$ \\\\\n45\tGPT-4o reg.\\ & --- & --- & ${<}0.001$ & ${<}0.02$ \\\\\n46\tQwen3 reg.\\ & --- & --- & ${<}0.001$ & ${<}0.02$ \\\\\n47\t\\bottomrule\n48\t\\end{tabular}\n49\t\\end{table}\n50\t\n51\t\\paragraph{Interaction test.}\n52\tTo verify that the additive GEE specification is appropriate, we test a style$\\times$originality interaction term on formality data for both judges.\n53\tThe interaction is non-significant in both cases (GPT-4o: $p = 0.53$; Qwen3-32B: $p = 0.32$), confirming that the additive model is the correct specification and that style and originality effects operate independently.\n54\t\n55\t\\paragraph{95\\% confidence intervals.}\n56\tTable~\\ref{tab:gee_ci} reports 95\\% CIs for all GEE odds ratios in Table~\\ref{tab:gee}.\n57\t\n58\t\\begin{table}[h]\n59\t\\centering\n60\t\\small\n61\t\\caption{95\\% CIs for GEE conditional decomposition (Table~\\ref{tab:gee}).}\n62\t\\label{tab:gee_ci}\n63\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}\n64\t\\toprule\n65\tConfig & Style OR [CI] & Orig OR [CI] \\\\\n66\t\\midrule\n67\tQwen3 verb.\\ & 13.10 [5.62, 30.55] & 0.49 [0.21, 1.13] \\\\\n68\tGPT-4o verb.\\ & 11.34 & 0.45 \\\\\n69\tGPT-4o form.\\ & 2.58 & 2.32 \\\\\n70\tQwen3 form.\\ & 4.20 [1.99, 8.87] & 4.70 \\\\\n71\tQwen3 form.$^a$ & 2.15 [1.46, 3.16] & 4.99 [3.40, 7.34] \\\\\n72\tGPT-4o reg.\\ & 1.88 & 2.21 \\\\\n73\tQwen3 reg.\\ & 1.91 [1.01, 3.58] & 5.72 [3.02, 10.82] \\\\\n74\t\\bottomrule\n75\t\\multicolumn{3}{l}{\\footnotesize $^a$ $n = 355$; other rows: $n = 88$.}\n76\t\\end{tabular}\n77\t\\end{table}\n78\t\n79\t\\section{Rewriter Independence}\n80\t\\label{app:rewriter_indep}\n81\t\n82\tTo test whether the Blindspot depends on the rewriter's identity, we replace Qwen3-32B with GPT-4o as rewriter while keeping Qwen3-32B as judge.\n83\t\n84\t\\paragraph{Verbosity (Table~\\ref{tab:rewriter_indep}).}\n85\tThe Style OR decreases by $22\\%$ ($13.10 \\to 10.17$), bounding evaluator-producer inflation of the style effect; the pure-style pattern (Originality n.s.) and Blindspot (BT significant, Likert n.s.) both persist.\n86\t\n87\t\\begin{table}[h]\n88\t\\centering\n89\t\\small\n90\t\\caption{Rewriter independence test: verbosity axis, Qwen3-32B judge. GPT-4o rewriter eliminates evaluator-producer overlap while preserving the Blindspot.}\n91\t\\label{tab:rewriter_indep}\n92\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n93\t\\toprule\n94\t & GPT-4o Rewriter & Qwen3 Rewriter \\\\\n95\t\\midrule\n96\tNLI pass rate & 81/100 (81\\%) & 86/100 (86\\%) \\\\\n97\tBT $P$ [95\\% CI] & .735 [.654, .809] & .773 [.703, .843] \\\\\n98\tLikert ATE ($p$) & .000 (.838) & ${\\sim}$0 (.179) \\\\\n99\tBlindspot & Yes & Yes \\\\\n100\tGEE Style OR ($p$) & 10.17 (${<}.0001$) & 13.10 (${<}.0001$) \\\\\n101\tGEE Orig OR ($p$) & 1.61 (n.s.) & 0.49 (n.s.) \\\\\n102\t\\bottomrule\n103\t\\end{tabular}\n104\t\\end{table}\n105\t\n106\t\\paragraph{Formality (Table~\\ref{tab:rewriter_indep_form}).}\n107\tGPT-4o rewriter produces formality-manipulated pairs yielding BT $64.4\\%$ $[56.1, 72.8]$, statistically indistinguishable from the Qwen3-32B baseline ($66.5\\%$ $[58.5, 73.9]$; $\\Delta = -2.1$pp).\n108\tThe Likert Blindspot replicates with the independent rewriter ($p = 0.184$, n.s.).\n109\t\n110\t\\begin{table}[h]\n111\t\\centering\n112\t\\small\n113\t\\caption{Rewriter independence test: formality axis, Qwen3-32B judge.}\n114\t\\label{tab:rewriter_indep_form}\n115\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n116\t\\toprule\n117\t & GPT-4o Rewriter & Qwen3 Rewriter \\\\\n118\t\\midrule\n119\tNLI pass rate & 90/100 (90\\%) & 88/100 (88\\%) \\\\\n120\tBT $P$ [95\\% CI] & .644 [.561, .728] & .665 [.585, .739] \\\\\n121\tLikert $d$ ($p$) & $-$.081 (.184) & .011 (.908) \\\\\n122\tBlindspot & Yes & Yes \\\\\n123\t\\bottomrule\n124\t\\end{tabular}\n125\t\\end{table}\n126\t\n127\t\\section{NLI Threshold Sensitivity Sweep}\n128\t\\label{app:nli_sweep}\n129\t\n130\tTable~\\ref{tab:nli_sweep} reports BT win probabilities across 11 NLI entailment thresholds for all four judge$\\times$axis configurations.\n131\tAll 44 combinations are significant ($p < 0.05$), and within each configuration BT varies by at most 3pp, ruling out threshold selection bias.\n132\tThe stability reflects the bimodal NLI score distribution: most pairs score above 0.95 or below 0.30, so thresholds between 0.50 and 0.95 admit nearly identical pair sets.\n133\t\n134\t\\begin{table}[h]\n135\t\\centering\n136\t\\small\n137\t\\caption{BT win probability across NLI thresholds. All 44 cells significant ($p < 0.05$). $n$: pairs passing threshold.}\n138\t\\label{tab:nli_sweep}\n139\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}rcccc@{}}\n140\t\\toprule\n141\tThresh & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{Formality BT} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{Verbosity BT} \\\\\n142\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \\cmidrule(lr){4-5}\n143\t & Qwen3 & GPT-4o & Qwen3 & GPT-4o \\\\\n144\t\\midrule\n145\t.50 & .648 & .615 & .770 & .768 \\\\\n146\t.55 & .648 & .615 & .770 & .768 \\\\\n147\t.60 & .656 & .621 & .770 & .768 \\\\\n148\t.65 & .656 & .621 & .767 & .766 \\\\\n149\t.70 & .657 & .623 & .767 & .766 \\\\\n150\t.75 & .657 & .623 & .773 & .760 \\\\\n151\t.80 & .657 & .623 & .773 & .760 \\\\\n152\t.85 & .657 & .623 & .773 & .760 \\\\\n153\t.90 & .665 & .630 & .773 & .760 \\\\\n154\t.95 & .653 & .629 & .765 & .752 \\\\\n155\t.98 & .649 & .642 & .771 & .769 \\\\\n156\t\\bottomrule\n157\t\\end{tabular}\n158\t\\end{table}\n159\t\n160\t\\section{Direction-Aware Gate Validation}\n161\t\\label{app:gate_validation}\n162\t\n163\tFor verbosity, the direction-aware NLI gate admits 21 additional pairs (32\\%) compared to strict bidirectional verification.\n164\tThese pairs show consistent preference direction (BT 73.8\\% vs.\\ 78.5\\% for bidirectional-pass pairs), and overall BT estimates are statistically indistinguishable ($\\Delta = {-}1.1$pp, 95\\% CI $[{-}5.6, {+}3.0]$), confirming that direction-aware gating recovers valid pairs without inflating the effect.\n165\tFor formality, strict bidirectional verification is appropriate: a relaxed (direction-aware) gate would admit 9 additional pairs with reversed preference (BT 33.4\\%), indicating content preservation failure rather than genuine style preference.\n166\tThis asymmetry empirically justifies the axis-dependent gating strategy described in Section~\\ref{sec:nli_gate}.\n167\t\n168\t\\section{Claude Sonnet 4 Equivalence Testing}\n169\t\\label{app:claude_tost}\n170\t\n171\tWe failed to detect style bias for Claude at the current sample size ($n = 87$; BT $p = 0.091$, $p = 0.284$).\n172\tFormal equivalence testing \\citep[TOST;][]{lakens2017equivalence} at $\\Delta = 10$pp is underpowered at $n = 87$; at $\\Delta = 15$pp (corresponding to a small-to-medium effect in evaluation contexts) both axes confirm equivalence, bounding any Claude style preference to ${<}15$pp---substantively distinct from GPT-4o ($13$--$23$pp) and Qwen3-32B ($17$--$27$pp).\n173\t\n174\t\\section{Bradley-Terry Goodness of Fit and Stratified Analysis}\n175\t\\label{app:bt_gof}\n176\t\n177\tBradley-Terry goodness-of-fit tests confirm adequate model fit for both axes (formality: deviance $= 52.80$, $\\text{df} = 45$, $p = 0.198$; verbosity: $p = 0.905$).\n178\tStratified analysis of consistent pairs by original-text direction reveals no preference heterogeneity for verbosity (Fisher $p = 0.453$) but significant direction asymmetry for formality ($88.9\\%$ vs.\\ $52.6\\%$, Fisher $p = 0.015$), independently confirming the originality confound structure identified by GEE (Table~\\ref{tab:gee}).\n179\t\n180\tTo assess robustness to originality confounding, we restrict to pairs where the judge assigned equal Likert scores to original and counterfactual texts (eliminating detectable quality differences).\n181\tStyle preference persists: formality BT $= 67.3\\%$ ($n = 55$, $\\Delta = {+}0.8$pp from full sample, 95\\% CI $[{-}11.0, {+}12.6]$); verbosity BT $= 76.0\\%$ ($n = 50$, $\\Delta = {-}1.3$pp, 95\\% CI $[{-}13.3, {+}10.3]$).\n182\tIn the anti-originality subset (pairs where the original never wins), effects strengthen (formality $75.0\\%$, verbosity $86.1\\%$), confirming that style preference is not an artifact of originality confounding.\n183\t\n184\t\\section{Bradley-Terry Confidence Intervals}\n185\t\\label{app:bt_ci}\n186\t\n187\tTable~\\ref{tab:bt_ci} reports bootstrap 95\\% confidence intervals for all Bradley-Terry estimates in Table~\\ref{tab:main_results}.\n188\t\n189\t\\begin{table}[h]\n190\t\\centering\n191\t\\small\n192\t\\caption{Bootstrap 95\\% CIs for BT win probabilities (10{,}000 pair-level resamples).}\n193\t\\label{tab:bt_ci}\n194\t\\begin{tabular}{lllc}\n195\t\\toprule\n196\tBench & Judge & Axis & BT $P$ [95\\% CI] \\\\\n197\t\\midrule\n198\tLLMBar & GPT-4o & Form.\\ & .630 [.557, .703] \\\\\n199\t & GPT-4o & Verb.\\ & .760 [.667, .853] \\\\\n200\t & GPT-4o & Reg.\\ & .570 [.468, .673] \\\\\n201\t\\cmidrule{2-4}\n202\t & Qwen3 & Form.\\ & .665 [.585, .739] \\\\\n203\t & Qwen3 & Verb.\\ & .773 [.703, .843] \\\\\n204\t & Qwen3 & Reg.\\ & .553 [.479, .628] \\\\\n205\t\\cmidrule{2-4}\n206\t & Claude & Form.\\ & .562 [.489, .636] \\\\\n207\t & Claude & Verb.\\ & .541 [.459, .616] \\\\\n208\t\\midrule\n209\tAEval & GPT-4o & Form.\\ & .727 [.663, .785] \\\\\n210\t & Qwen3 & Form.\\ & .657 [.581, .733] \\\\\n211\t & Qwen3 & Verb.\\ & .841 [.774, .902] \\\\\n212\t\\midrule\n213\tMTB & GPT-4o & Form.\\ & .692 [.623, .753] \\\\\n214\t & Qwen3 & Form.\\ & .712 [.630, .788] \\\\\n215\t\\bottomrule\n216\t\\end{tabular}\n217\t\\end{table}\n218\t\n219\t\\section{Bootstrap Attenuation Analysis}\n220\t\\label{app:bootstrap_attenuation}\n221\t\n222\tTo distinguish measurement attenuation from insufficient statistical power, we subsample the $n = 355$ formality replication (Qwen3-32B) at seven sample sizes and compute Likert Cohen's $d$ with bootstrap 95\\% CIs at each (Table~\\ref{tab:attenuation}).\n223\tUnder a power limitation, $d$ would remain constant while only $p$-values improve with larger $n$; under measurement attenuation, $d$ itself converges to zero.\n224\tThe data support attenuation: $d_{\\text{median}} = {-}0.017$ across all sample sizes, with CIs narrowing from ${\\pm}0.3$ to ${\\pm}0.1$ but never excluding zero.\n225\tThe same data yield BT $= 66.5\\%$ ($p < 0.0001$), confirming that the pairwise signal is robust.\n226\tVerbosity shows a similar pattern ($d \\approx 0.15$--$0.19$, stable, CIs always containing zero; BT $= 76$--$77\\%$, $p < 0.0001$).\n227\tWithin the agnostic framework of \\S\\ref{sec:likert_pairwise}, this evidence favors the measurement attenuation interpretation but does not exclude construct divergence.\n228\t\n229\t\\begin{table}[h]\n230\t\\centering\n231\t\\small\n232\t\\caption{Bootstrap attenuation analysis: formality $\\times$ Qwen3-32B. Likert $d$ remains flat near zero across sample sizes while BT is stable at 66.5\\%.}\n233\t\\label{tab:attenuation}\n234\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}rccc@{}}\n235\t\\toprule\n236\t$n$ & Likert $d$ & 95\\% CI & Contains 0? \\\\\n237\t\\midrule\n238\t50 & $-$0.017 & [$-$0.308, 0.262] & Yes \\\\\n239\t100 & $-$0.015 & [$-$0.219, 0.176] & Yes \\\\\n240\t150 & $-$0.019 & [$-$0.179, 0.145] & Yes \\\\\n241\t200 & $-$0.016 & [$-$0.157, 0.119] & Yes \\\\\n242\t250 & $-$0.018 & [$-$0.144, 0.107] & Yes \\\\\n243\t300 & $-$0.018 & [$-$0.131, 0.095] & Yes \\\\\n244\t355 & $-$0.017 & [$-$0.123, 0.087] & Yes \\\\\n245\t\\bottomrule\n246\t\\end{tabular}\n247\t\\end{table}\n248\t\n249\t\\section{Verbosity Sensitivity Hierarchy}\n250\t\\label{app:verb_sensitivity}\n251\t\n252\tTable~\\ref{tab:verb_sensitivity} replicates the seven-method sensitivity analysis (Table~\\ref{tab:sensitivity}) on the verbosity axis ($n = 86$ NLI-filtered pairs, Qwen3-32B judge, LLMBar).\n253\tThe attenuation pattern is more extreme than formality: all six Likert-based methods remain non-significant (lowest $p = 0.213$) despite a stronger pairwise signal (BT 77.3\\% vs.\\ 66.5\\% for formality), and Likert effect sizes ($d = 0.036$--$0.056$) are near zero.\n254\t\n255\t\\begin{table}[h]\n256\t\\centering\n257\t\\small\n258\t\\caption{Sensitivity hierarchy for verbosity ($n = 86$, Qwen3-32B, LLMBar). All Likert methods fail; only BT reaches significance.}\n259\t\\label{tab:verb_sensitivity}\n260\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n261\t\\toprule\n262\tMethod & Statistic & $p$ & Sig? \\\\\n263\t\\midrule\n264\tBT model & 77.3\\% $[70.3, 84.3]$ & ${<}0.001$ & Yes \\\\\n265\t\\midrule\n266\tLikert ATE & $d = 0.056$ & 0.691 & No \\\\\n267\tBinarized $\\chi^2$ & OR $= 2.16$ & 0.213 & No \\\\\n268\tOrdinal reg.\\ & $\\beta = 0.159$ & 0.575 & No \\\\\n269\tPaired $t$-test & $d = 0.056$ & 0.607 & No \\\\\n270\tMann-Whitney $U$ & $r = {-}0.046$ & 0.578 & No \\\\\n271\tCohen's $d$ & $d = 0.036$ & --- & No \\\\\n272\t\\bottomrule\n273\t\\multicolumn{4}{p{0.9\\columnwidth}}{\\footnotesize Effect sizes use style-aligned scoring (verbose $-$ concise), isolating style preference direction. Table~\\ref{tab:main_results} reports RATE-compatible ATE (counterfactual $-$ original), which conflates style and originality effects.}\n274\t\\end{tabular}\n275\t\\end{table}\n276\t\n277\t\\section{100-Point Continuous Likert Scale}\n278\t\\label{app:likert100}\n279\t\n280\tTo test whether the Blindspot reflects integer-scale granularity rather than a paradigm difference between absolute and relative evaluation, we replicate the formality and verbosity experiments using a 100-point continuous scale ($0$--$100$) with Qwen3-32B as judge (Table~\\ref{tab:likert100}).\n281\tBoth axes remain non-significant: formality $p = 0.725$ (tie rate $65.9\\%$) and verbosity $p = 0.117$ (tie rate $51.2\\%$), while the same data yield BT $66.5\\%$ ($p < 0.0001$) and $77.3\\%$ ($p < 0.0001$) respectively.\n282\tThe persistence of high tie rates even with 101 possible values confirms that LLM judges do not exploit finer granularity to express style preferences; the Blindspot arises from absolute-vs-relative evaluation paradigms, not scale resolution.\n283\t\n284\t\\begin{table}[h]\n285\t\\centering\n286\t\\small\n287\t\\caption{100-point continuous Likert vs.\\ pairwise (BT). The Blindspot persists despite 101 possible score values.}\n288\t\\label{tab:likert100}\n289\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\n290\t\\toprule\n291\tAxis & Likert $p$ & Tie rate & BT $P$ & BT $p$ \\\\\n292\t\\midrule\n293\tFormality & .725 & 65.9\\% & .665 & ${<}.0001$ \\\\\n294\tVerbosity & .117 & 51.2\\% & .773 & ${<}.0001$ \\\\\n295\t\\bottomrule\n296\t\\end{tabular}\n297\t\\end{table}\n298\t\n299\t\\section{NLI Gate Ablation}\n300\t\\label{app:nli_ablation}\n301\t\n302\tTable~\\ref{tab:nli_ablation} compares BT estimates across NLI-pass, NLI-fail, and all pairs.\n303\tFor formality, NLI-fail pairs reverse to 41.7\\% (preferring casual over formal), demonstrating that the gate removes content-confounded pairs rather than amplifying style signal.\n304\tThe small NLI-fail sample ($n \\approx 12$) limits precision; this serves as a bounding analysis.\n305\tFor verbosity, NLI-fail pairs remain at 75.0\\%, consistent with the dominant style effect (OR $= 13.10$) overwhelming content noise.\n306\t\n307\t\\begin{table}[h]\n308\t\\centering\n309\t\\small\n310\t\\caption{NLI gate ablation: BT win probability by NLI subset.}\n311\t\\label{tab:nli_ablation}\n312\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n313\t\\toprule\n314\tAxis & NLI-pass & NLI-fail & All \\\\\n315\t\\midrule\n316\tFormality & 66.5\\% & 41.7\\% & 63.0\\% \\\\\n317\tVerbosity & 77.3\\% & 75.0\\% & 76.0\\% \\\\\n318\t\\bottomrule\n319\t\\end{tabular}\n320\t\\end{table}\n321\t\n322\t\\section{Rewriting Artifact Control}\n323\t\\label{app:artifact_control}\n324\t\n325\tSame-style double-rewrite (DR) controls quantify the rewriting artifact using BT models (consistent with all other pairwise analyses).\n326\tTable~\\ref{tab:artifact} reports BT $P(\\text{original})$---the probability the judge prefers the original over its same-style DR.\n327\tBoth judges systematically prefer LLM-polished text across axes; for verbosity, BT $P(\\text{original}) < 0.17$ indicates that rewriting quality dominates when content is held constant, validating the GEE decomposition that separates style from artifact effects.\n328\t\n329\t\\begin{table}[h]\n330\t\\centering\n331\t\\small\n332\t\\caption{Rewriting artifact: BT $P(\\text{orig})$ vs.\\ same-style double-rewrite (bootstrap 95\\% CI, 10{,}000 resamples). Values ${<}0.5$: judges prefer LLM-polished text.}\n333\t\\label{tab:artifact}\n334\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llcc@{}}\n335\t\\toprule\n336\tAxis & Judge & BT $P(\\text{orig})$ [CI] & $p$ \\\\\n337\t\\midrule\n338\tForm.\\ & GPT-4o & .418 [.342, .500] & .037 \\\\\n339\t & Qwen3 & .377 [.303, .451] & .0006 \\\\\n340\t\\midrule\n341\tVerb.\\ & GPT-4o & .167 [.084, .262] & ${<}.0001$ \\\\\n342\t & Qwen3 & .082 [.035, .140] & ${<}.0001$ \\\\\n343\t\\bottomrule\n344\t\\end{tabular}\n345\t\\end{table}\n346\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Wj2CiaHKUBUwsCGmV3oRYB","session_id":"84c1e33b-a907-4d62-a6bc-d61bef62f953","uuid":"58d4bbbc-adc5-4f16-a251-8dd2aefdc786","timestamp":"2026-05-21T16:11:56.854Z"}
