{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017tYnYZapETK8sr64esnQBi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MLHodKWfjJfY2uF6SRgB67","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_5/projects/causal_style_decomp_judge/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T152517/best/paper.tex","limit":100},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14968,"cache_read_input_tokens":59511,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":14968,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Wj2CiaHKUBUwsCGmV3oRYB","session_id":"84c1e33b-a907-4d62-a6bc-d61bef62f953","uuid":"6072eb9b-e0a9-4e33-8f60-57feb3d0b01d"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MLHodKWfjJfY2uF6SRgB67","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,twocolumn]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{times}\n5\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n6\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n7\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n8\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n9\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n12\t\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n13\t\\usepackage{caption}\n14\t\\usepackage{tablefootnote}\n15\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n16\t\\setlist{nosep,leftmargin=*}\n17\t\n18\t\\captionsetup{font=small,labelfont=bf}\n19\t\n20\t\\title{The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias\\\\in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition}\n21\t\n22\t\\author{Anonymous}\n23\t\\date{}\n24\t\n25\t\\begin{document}\n26\t\\maketitle\n27\t\n28\t\\begin{abstract}\n29\tLLM-as-judge evaluation is now core infrastructure for NLP benchmarks and alignment pipelines, yet standard Likert-based auditing systematically misses style-driven preferences.\n30\tWe introduce \\textbf{Controlled Style Decomposition (CSD)}, a black-box diagnostic that combines NLI-gated content control, per-axis style manipulation, and position-controlled pairwise comparison to isolate style bias in LLM judges.\n31\tAcross three independent judge architectures (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B, Llama-3.3-70B), three benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench), and three style axes (formality, verbosity, register), pairwise comparison reliably detects bias (Bradley-Terry $P = 63$--$77\\%$) that \\emph{all} seven Likert-based statistical methods fail to detect ($d \\to 0$).\n32\tA 100-point fine-grained Likert ablation confirms that the blindspot arises from measurement mode (absolute vs.\\ comparative), not scale granularity.\n33\tGEE decomposition further reveals three qualitatively distinct confound regimes---verbosity is a pure style bias, formality is a conditional effect requiring decomposition, and register is originality-dominated---providing prescriptive guidance on when bias estimates are directly interpretable.\n34\tFive independent controls bound the risk of format-level amplification.\n35\tClaude Sonnet serves as a negative control, confirming CSD does not fabricate false positives.\n36\t\\end{abstract}\n37\t\n38\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n39\t\\section{Introduction}\n40\t\\label{sec:intro}\n41\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n42\t\n43\tLLM-based evaluation has become the default paradigm for both benchmark construction and reward modeling in alignment pipelines \\cite{zheng2024judging,dubois2024alpacafarm}. Style bias---the tendency of LLM judges to prefer stylistic properties such as verbosity or formality over substantive quality---has emerged as the single strongest predictor of judge preference, with recent evidence showing style explains $R = 0.999$ of variance in preference-optimized outputs \\cite{wu2024sosbench}. Yet the standard toolkit for auditing such bias relies on Likert-based absolute scoring, which, as we demonstrate, is structurally blind to the very biases it is meant to detect.\n44\t\n45\tTwo critical gaps remain in the literature. First, no prior work systematically compares measurement modes (Likert vs.\\ pairwise) on \\emph{identical} content-controlled data to establish whether discrepancies reflect statistical power or a structural measurement limitation. Second, existing approaches treat style bias as monolithic: either present or absent. They do not characterize the \\emph{per-axis confound structure} that determines whether a detected bias is directly interpretable, conditionally interpretable, or dominated by non-style confounds.\n46\t\n47\tWe address both gaps with \\textbf{Controlled Style Decomposition (CSD)}, a pipeline that: (1)~generates counterfactual text pairs differing along a single style axis while preserving semantic content via NLI gating; (2)~evaluates each pair under both Likert and pairwise protocols with position balancing; and (3)~decomposes observed preferences via GEE to separate style effects from originality confounds.\n48\t\n49\tOur dual contribution is:\n50\t\n51\t\\begin{enumerate}\n52\t\\item \\textbf{The Likert Blindspot} (\\S\\ref{sec:blindspot}): Pairwise comparison detects style bias (BT 63--77\\%) that all seven Likert methods miss ($|d| < 0.15$, all $p > 0.17$). A 100-point Likert ablation rules out scale granularity as the cause, establishing measurement mode as the dominant factor. We present two candidate interpretations---measurement attenuation and construct divergence---and show both yield the same operational conclusion: Likert-only auditing is insufficient for style bias.\n53\t\n54\t\\item \\textbf{Per-axis confound structure} (\\S\\ref{sec:confound}): GEE decomposition reveals three qualitatively distinct regimes that replicate across all three judge architectures: verbosity is a \\emph{pure} style bias (Style OR 9.5--13.1, Orig OR NS), formality is \\emph{conditional} (both significant), and register is \\emph{originality-dominated}. This structure is a property of the data, not the judge, and provides prescriptive guidance for practitioners.\n55\t\\end{enumerate}\n56\t\n57\tAdditionally, we demonstrate cross-architecture generalizability across three independent model families (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B, Llama-3.3-70B), cross-benchmark consistency across LLMBar, AlpacaEval, and MT-Bench, and rewriter independence across three rewriting models. Claude Sonnet serves as a negative control (BT 56.2\\%, $p = 0.091$), confirming CSD does not produce false positives. Five independent robustness controls bound the risk of format-level amplification (\\S\\ref{sec:robustness}).\n58\t\n59\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n60\t\\section{Related Work}\n61\t\\label{sec:related}\n62\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n63\t\n64\t\\paragraph{LLM-as-Judge Bias.}\n65\tStyle bias in LLM judges has been documented along individual axes: verbosity \\cite{saito2023verbosity,wu2024sosbench}, sycophancy \\cite{sharma2024towards}, and presentation effects \\cite{park2024offsetbias}. SOS-Bench \\cite{wu2024sosbench} shows style is the dominant factor ($R = 0.999$) but uses correlational analysis without content control. CASSE \\cite{chen2024casse} manipulates multiple style dimensions simultaneously but assumes feature independence without empirical validation. Our CSD pipeline provides per-axis isolation with content control via NLI gating.\n66\t\n67\t\\paragraph{Measurement Methodology.}\n68\tRATE \\cite{stureborg2024rate} introduces Likert-based ATE estimation for LLM judge sensitivity, but operates exclusively in the Likert regime. LC-AlpacaEval \\cite{dubois2024lengthcontrolled} addresses length bias via logistic regression in pairwise mode. The ``Comparative Trap'' \\cite{jeong2025comparative} argues pairwise formats amplify biases. We provide bounded detection with five independent controls that address amplification concerns.\n69\t\n70\t\\paragraph{Psychometric Foundations.}\n71\tThe greater sensitivity of comparative vs.\\ absolute judgments is classical (Thurstone, 1927; Stevens, 1946). We provide the first empirical quantification in the LLM judge setting, including a 100-point Likert ablation that isolates measurement mode from scale granularity.\n72\t\n73\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n74\t\\section{Controlled Style Decomposition}\n75\t\\label{sec:method}\n76\t%------------------------------------------------------------\n77\t\n78\tCSD is a three-stage pipeline for diagnosing per-axis style bias in any black-box LLM judge.\n79\t\n80\t\\subsection{Stage 1: Content-Controlled Style Manipulation}\n81\t\\label{sec:manipulation}\n82\t\n83\tGiven a source text $x$, we generate a counterfactual $x'$ that differs along exactly one style axis $a \\in \\{\\text{formality, verbosity, register}\\}$ while preserving semantic content. We produce bidirectional rewrites: for formality, both formal$\\to$casual and casual$\\to$formal; for verbosity, both verbose$\\to$concise and concise$\\to$verbose.\n84\t\n85\t\\paragraph{NLI Gating.}\n86\tTo enforce content preservation, we apply a pretrained NLI model to each $(x, x')$ pair. Only pairs classified as \\emph{entailment} in both directions ($x \\Rightarrow x'$ and $x' \\Rightarrow x$) pass the gate. This yields NLI pass rates of 88--92\\% across axes, filtering out pairs where the rewrite altered substantive content. The NLI gate is critical: without it, formality BT \\emph{reverses} from 66.5\\% to 41.7\\% (\\S\\ref{sec:nli_ablation}), demonstrating it removes content noise rather than amplifying style signal.\n87\t\n88\t\\subsection{Stage 2: Dual Measurement Protocol}\n89\t\\label{sec:dual}\n90\t\n91\tEach NLI-filtered pair is evaluated by the target judge under two protocols:\n92\t\n93\t\\paragraph{Pairwise comparison} with position balancing: each pair is presented in both orders (A-B and B-A). We fit a Bradley-Terry model to recover $P(\\text{style}^+)$, the probability that the styled variant is preferred. Bootstrap confidence intervals (10,000 resamples) provide inference.\n94\t\n95\t\\paragraph{Likert scoring:} each text is independently scored on a 7-point quality scale (or 100-point in the granularity ablation). We compute Cohen's $d$, ATE, paired $t$-test, Mann-Whitney $U$, ordinal logistic regression, and binarized $\\chi^2$ as complementary sensitivity measures.\n96\t\n97\tThe \\textbf{Blindspot} is defined as: BT significant ($p < 0.05$) \\emph{and} all Likert methods non-significant ($p > 0.05$, $|d| < 0.2$).\n98\t\n99\t\\subsection{Stage 3: GEE Confound Decomposition}\n100\t\\label{sec:gee}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Wj2CiaHKUBUwsCGmV3oRYB","session_id":"84c1e33b-a907-4d62-a6bc-d61bef62f953","uuid":"72bd8da0-e396-40c5-b98b-964516193c23","timestamp":"2026-05-21T16:12:15.436Z"}
