{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LCuKGyeb2wQ4TftJAL5oF9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Experiments}\n2\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n3\t\n4\t\n5\t\\subsection{Statistical Framework}\n6\t\\label{sec:stat_framework}\n7\t\n8\tWe designate a primary family of eight hypothesis tests (three judges $\\times$ formality and verbosity, plus register for GPT-4o and Qwen3-32B) and control the family-wise error rate with Holm-Bonferroni correction at $\\alpha = 0.05$.\n9\tTable~\\ref{tab:main_results} reports both raw and adjusted $p$-values.\n10\tAnalyses outside this grid are classified as either \\emph{exploratory} (NLI threshold sensitivity sweep, \\S\\ref{sec:nli_threshold}) or \\emph{confirmatory} (consistency filter validation, \\S\\ref{sec:consistency}).\n11\tExploratory analyses are reported with uncorrected $p$-values and interpreted as hypothesis-generating; confirmatory analyses test specific hypotheses about pipeline validity and are evaluated at the nominal $\\alpha = 0.05$ level without additional correction, as they address orthogonal questions about measurement reliability rather than the primary style-bias family.\n12\tBradley-Terry parameters are estimated via spectral initialization followed by minorization-maximization \\citep{hunter2004mm}, using the default regularization of the \\texttt{choix} Python library.\n13\tAll bootstrap confidence intervals use 10{,}000 pair-level resamples with seed 42.\n14\t\n15\t\\begin{table}[t]\n16\t\\centering\n17\t\\small\n18\t\\caption{Main results across four judges, three style axes, and three benchmarks. BT: Bradley-Terry win probability (10{,}000 pair-level bootstrap resamples; 95\\% CIs in Appendix~\\ref{app:bt_ci}). AEval: AlpacaEval; MTB: MT-Bench. Claude Sonnet 4 serves as negative control.}\n19\t\\label{tab:main_results}\n20\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lllccc@{}}\n21\t\\toprule\n22\tBench & Judge & Axis & BT $P$ & Likert $d$ ($p$) & \\\\\n23\t\\midrule\n24\tLLMBar & GPT-4o & Form.\\ & .630$^{***}$ & .102 (.332) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n25\t & GPT-4o & Verb.\\ & .760$^{***}$ & $-$.095 (.403) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n26\t & GPT-4o & Reg.\\ & .570$^{\\dagger}$ & $-$.031 (.785) & $\\dagger_{\\text{e}}$ \\\\\n27\t\\cmidrule{2-6}\n28\t & Qwen3 & Form.\\ & .665$^{***}$ & .011 (.908) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n29\t & Qwen3 & Form.$^b$ & .675$^{***}$ & $-$.025 (.600) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n30\t & Qwen3 & Verb.\\ & .773$^{***}$ & $-$.151 (.179) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n31\t & Qwen3 & Reg.\\ & .553 & $-$.073 (.505) & $\\dagger_{\\text{e}}$ \\\\\n32\t\\cmidrule{2-6}\n33\t & Llama-70B & Form.\\ & .695$^{***}$ & .010 (.846) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n34\t & Llama-70B & Verb.\\ & .735$^{***}$ & .020 (.752) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n35\t\\cmidrule{2-6}\n36\t & Claude & Form.\\ & .562 & $-$.021 (.721) & --- \\\\\n37\t & Claude & Verb.\\ & .541 & $-$.039 (.743) & --- \\\\\n38\t\\midrule\n39\tAEval & GPT-4o & Form.\\ & .727$^{***}$ & .148 (.170) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n40\t & Qwen3 & Form.\\ & .657$^{***}$ & .081 (.198) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n41\t & Qwen3 & Verb.\\ & .841$^{***}$ & .375 (.0003) & $\\ddagger$ \\\\\n42\t\\midrule\n43\tMTB & GPT-4o & Form.\\ & .692$^{***}$ & .167 (.016) & $\\ddagger$ \\\\\n44\t & Qwen3 & Form.\\ & .712$^{***}$ & .149 (.007) & $\\ddagger$ \\\\\n45\t\\bottomrule\n46\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\footnotesize $^{***}$Holm-adj.\\ $p < 0.001$;\\; $^{\\dagger}$raw $p < 0.05$, Holm-adj.\\ n.s.}\\\\\n47\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\footnotesize $\\dagger$: Blindspot;\\; $\\ddagger$: both sig.;\\; ---: neither;\\; $\\dagger_{\\text{e}}$: exploratory.}\\\\\n48\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\footnotesize $^b$ Scaled replication ($n = 355$); other LLMBar: $n = 88$.}\\\\\n49\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\footnotesize Negative $d$: judge rates style-manipulated variants lower on Likert.}\\\\\n50\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\footnotesize Empty cells: benchmark--axis combinations not tested; cross-benchmark}\\\\\n51\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\footnotesize validation focuses on strongest-evidence configurations.}\n52\t\\end{tabular}\n53\t\\end{table}\n54\t\n55\t\n56\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n57\t\\centering\n58\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/forest_plot.pdf}\n59\t\\caption{Bradley-Terry win probabilities across three target judges and three style axes. Filled diamonds: significant ($p < 0.05$); open diamonds: non-significant. Error bars: 95\\% bootstrap CI. The dashed line at 0.50 indicates no style preference. Verbosity shows the strongest and most consistent bias across all three judges.}\n60\t\\label{fig:forest_plot}\n61\t\\end{figure}\n62\t\n63\t\\section{Analysis}\n64\t\\label{sec:analysis}\n65\t\n66\t\n67\t\\subsection{NLI Gate Validation}\n68\t\\label{sec:nli_validation}\n69\t\n70\t\\paragraph{False negative rate.}\n71\tWe validate all 100 NLI decisions against an LLM annotator (Qwen3-32B) prompted to judge whether semantic content is preserved.\n72\tThe two systems agree on 91 of 100 pairs (Gwet's AC1 $= 0.89$; Cohen's $\\kappa = 0.43$ is depressed by prevalence skew \\citep{feinstein1990kappa}).\n73\tOf the 12 rejected pairs, 8 are judged content-preserving by the LLM annotator, yielding an overall false negative rate of $8/95 = 8.4\\%$.\n74\tThree robustness checks confirm these do not compromise conclusions: BT stable across 11 NLI thresholds from 0.50 to 0.98 (${\\leq}3$pp variation across all four judge$\\times$axis configurations; \\S\\ref{sec:nli_threshold}), no covariate differences between retained and discarded pairs (all $p > 0.12$; \\S\\ref{sec:consistency}), and no directional bias in false negatives (Fisher $p = 0.35$).\n75\tThe NLI gate operates as a precision-oriented conservative filter (precision $= 98.9\\%$); verbosity-rejected pairs have larger edit distances (Cliff's $\\delta = +0.45$, $p < 0.001$), making estimates lower bounds.\n76\tFull-scale GPT-4o validation on all 200 NLI gate decisions yields 92.1\\% pass precision for formality and 82.6\\% for verbosity, with the lower verbosity figure reflecting the permissiveness of direction-aware gating (17.4\\% false positives vs.\\ 7.9\\% for the strict bidirectional formality gate---a trade-off for the higher pass rate that enables adequate sample sizes).\n77\t\n78\t\\paragraph{Threshold sensitivity.}\n79\t\\label{sec:nli_threshold}\n80\tA continuous sweep across 11 NLI thresholds (0.50--0.98) confirms that BT estimates are robust to threshold choice: all 44 judge$\\times$axis$\\times$threshold combinations are significant, with maximum variation ${\\leq}3$pp within each configuration (Appendix~\\ref{app:nli_sweep}).\n81\tNLI scores follow a bimodal distribution (most pairs above 0.95 or below 0.30), so the 0.90 threshold lies on a plateau rather than a cliff edge.\n82\t\n83\t\n84\t\\subsection{Measurement Sensitivity: Likert vs.\\ Pairwise}\n85\t\\label{sec:likert_pairwise}\n86\t\n87\t\\paragraph{Head-to-head comparison with RATE.}\n88\tApplying RATE's Likert ATE methodology \\citep{reber2025rate} to our formality style-transformed pairs yields Cohen's $d = 0.10$, $p = 0.33$ (non-significant, $n = 88$); pairwise comparison on identical data reveals a 63.0\\% formal preference ($p = 0.0004$, Bradley-Terry on all 173 comparisons), confirmed by a consistency-filtered sign test (73.9\\%, $p = 0.006$, $n = 46$ consistent pairs).\n89\tThis is the Likert Blindspot: the same data, the same style intervention, two measurement instruments---one detects bias, the other does not.\n90\tA $4\\times$ scaled replication ($n = 355$) distinguishes measurement attenuation from insufficient power: the Likert effect size shrinks to $d = {-}0.025$ (near zero) while BT remains stable at $67.5\\%$ ($p < 0.0001$)---if the gap were merely statistical power, $d$ would remain non-zero at larger $n$.\n91\tBootstrap subsampling across seven sample sizes confirms this pattern: $d$ remains flat near zero ($d_{\\text{median}} = {-}0.017$) as CIs narrow from ${\\pm}0.3$ to ${\\pm}0.1$, never excluding zero (Appendix~\\ref{app:bootstrap_attenuation}).\n92\tAttenuation follows a severity spectrum.\n93\tAt moderate bias (BT 63--67\\%), it is complete: $d$ converges to ${\\approx}0$ even at $n = 355$, rendering the bias invisible to any Likert-based test.\n94\tAt stronger effects (BT ${>}70\\%$), Likert partially detects but severely attenuates: MT-Bench formality yields Likert $d \\approx 0.15$ for BT ${\\sim}70\\%$ effects---${>}60\\%$ signal compression---explaining why three of nine bias-positive cells escape the Blindspot (Table~\\ref{tab:main_results}, $\\ddagger$ rows).\n95\t\n96\tThe two frameworks are complementary: RATE measures aggregate quality effects via Likert ATE; CSD targets per-axis style preference via forced-choice comparison, which eliminates ties and is more sensitive to ordinal preferences.\n97\tThe Blindspot arises because Likert tie rates cluster at 58--73\\% across all eight judge--axis configurations.\n98\tA 100-point continuous scale ($0$--$100$) does not resolve this: formality remains non-significant ($p = 0.725$, tie rate $65.9\\%$) and verbosity likewise ($p = 0.117$, tie rate $51.2\\%$; Appendix~\\ref{app:likert100}), demonstrating that the Blindspot reflects a paradigm difference between absolute and relative evaluation rather than scale granularity.\n99\t\n100\t\n101\t\\paragraph{Sensitivity hierarchy across measurement methods.}\n102\tTo confirm that the Likert Blindspot is not an artifact of a particular statistical test, we apply seven measurement methods to the same formality data and rank them by sensitivity (Table~\\ref{tab:sensitivity}).\n103\tBoth pairwise methods detect the formality preference; all Likert-based methods fail regardless of whether they assume interval scaling (ATE $t$-test), ordinal scaling (ordinal logistic regression), or nonparametric ranking (Wilcoxon signed-rank).\n104\tThe same attenuation pattern replicates on verbosity (Appendix~\\ref{app:verb_sensitivity}), where all six Likert methods remain non-significant despite a stronger pairwise signal (BT 77.3\\%, $p < 0.001$).\n105\t\n106\t\\begin{table}[t]\n107\t\\centering\n108\t\\small\n109\t\\caption{Sensitivity hierarchy: seven measurement methods applied to the same formality style-transformed data. Only pairwise methods (top two rows) reach significance. All Likert-based methods fail.}\n110\t\\label{tab:sensitivity}\n111\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n112\t\\toprule\n113\tMethod & Statistic & $p$ & Data \\\\\n114\t\\midrule\n115\tBT model & 63.0\\% $[55.7, 70.3]$ & $0.0004$ & All 173 comp. \\\\\n116\tSign test (filtered) & 73.9\\% (34/46) & 0.006 & Consistent \\\\\n117\t\\midrule\n118\tLikert ATE ($t$-test) & $d = 0.102$ & 0.332 & NLI$\\geq$0.90 \\\\\n119\tBinarized Likert & 60.6\\% & 0.296 & Non-tied \\\\\n120\tOrdinal reg.\\ (prop.\\ odds) & $\\beta = {-}0.079$ & 0.769 & NLI$\\geq$0.90 \\\\\n121\tGEE (exchangeable) & $\\beta = {-}0.084$ & 0.587 & NLI$\\geq$0.90 \\\\\n122\tWilcoxon signed-rank & $W = 274$ & 0.905 & NLI$\\geq$0.90 \\\\\n123\t\\bottomrule\n124\t\\end{tabular}\n125\t\\end{table}\n126\t\n127\tFigure~\\ref{fig:power_curve} plots detection probability curves at observed effect magnitudes (not post-hoc power; cf.\\ \\citealt{hoenig2001abuse}): Likert requires ${\\approx}4.5\\times$ more data to detect the same bias.\n128\t\\paragraph{Measurement attenuation vs.\\ construct divergence.}\n129\tAn equally plausible interpretation is that Likert and pairwise modes measure fundamentally different constructs: Likert captures absolute quality difference while pairwise captures ordinal preference.\n130\tUnder this view, the divergence reflects construct difference rather than measurement failure---the two formats answer different questions about the same data.\n131\tWe cannot definitively distinguish these interpretations; however, both lead to the same practical conclusion: practitioners relying on Likert audits will systematically miss style preferences that drive pairwise-based alignment pipelines (RLHF, DPO), regardless of whether the gap arises from attenuation or construct mismatch.\n132\t\n133\t\\paragraph{Detection vs.\\ amplification.}\n134\t\\label{sec:amplification}\n135\t\\citet{jeong2025comparative} show that pairwise comparison amplifies style biases (the Comparative Trap).\n136\tWhile format-level amplification of micro-preferences cannot be fully excluded, six independent controls bound this risk: (i)~NLI gate ablation reverses formality NLI-fail pairs to BT 41.7\\%---the gate removes content contamination, not preserves amplified signal (Appendix~\\ref{app:nli_ablation}); (ii)~Claude Sonnet 4, evaluated with the identical pairwise format, shows no bias (BT 56.2\\%/54.1\\%, n.s.); (iii)~cross-axis BT variation (register 55\\% $<$ formality 67\\% $<$ verbosity 77\\%) is inconsistent with uniform format amplification; (iv)~the rewriting artifact, quantified by placebo controls (Appendix~\\ref{app:artifact_control}), is absorbed by GEE decomposition; (v)~debiased prompting partially reduces bias (verbosity $-13.1$pp; formality $-5.7$/$-0.4$pp), confirming judge preferences rather than format mechanics; (vi)~three independent architectures (proprietary API, open 32B, open 70B) all confirm the Blindspot with consistent GEE confound structure, ruling out architecture-specific artifacts.\n137\t\n138\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n139\t\\centering\n140\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/power_curve.pdf}\n141\t\\caption{Detection probability at observed effect magnitudes. 80\\% power crossings: sign test $n \\approx 62$, BT $n \\approx 114$, binarized Likert $n \\approx 462$, Likert ATE $n \\approx 1{,}473$. At typical scales ($n = 50$--$200$, shaded), pairwise methods reach 80\\% power while Likert methods remain far below. At $n = 355$, Likert $d = {-}0.025$ confirms the gap is structural, not merely a power issue.}\n142\t\\label{fig:power_curve}\n143\t\\end{figure}\n144\t\n145\t\\subsection{Cross-Judge Replication}\n146\t\\label{sec:cross_judge}\n147\t\n148\tTwo independent judges replicate the Blindspot: Qwen3-32B (BT 66.5\\%, $p < 0.0001$; Likert $d = 0.011$, $p = 0.908$) and Llama-3.3-70B (BT 69.5\\%, $p = 0.0001$; Likert $d = 0.010$, $p = 0.846$).\n149\tThe three target judges span independent architectures (proprietary API, open-weight 32B, open-weight 70B), ruling out architecture-specific artifacts.\n150\tQwen3-32B's higher GEE estimate may partly reflect evaluator-producer bias (it serves as both rewriter and judge; Appendix~\\ref{app:rewriter_indep}).\n151\t\n152\t\\subsection{Cross-Axis Replication: Verbosity}\n153\t\\label{sec:cross_axis}\n154\t\n155\tThe verbosity axis provides the strongest and cleanest demonstration of the Blindspot.\n156\tAll three target judges show consistent verbosity preference: GPT-4o 76.0\\% ($p = 0.0001$), Qwen3-32B 77.3\\% ($p < 0.0001$), and Llama-70B 73.5\\% ($p = 0.0001$), while Likert ATE remains non-significant for all three ($p = 0.403$, $0.179$, $0.752$).\n157\tUnlike formality, verbosity is style-dominated across all three judges: GEE decomposition confirms massive style effects (OR $= 8.2$--$13.1$, all $p < .0001$) while originality ORs are below 1 ($0.38$--$0.49$), indicating any artifact favors rewrites rather than originals (Table~\\ref{tab:gee}).\n158\tBoth rewriting directions favor verbose responses, with the concise$\\to$verbose direction (90.7\\%) even exceeding verbose$\\to$concise (80.6\\%), providing convergent directional and GEE evidence that the full 77.3\\% effect is attributable to style rather than originality.\n159\tControlling for length (ratio, difference, log-ratio) leaves the style OR virtually unchanged ($<$2\\%; all $p > 0.27$).\n160\t\n161\t\\subsection{Register: Weak Evidence with Strong Originality Confound}\n162\t\\label{sec:negative_control}\n163\t\n164\tRegister provides weak but directionally consistent evidence (GPT-4o BT 57.0\\%, $p = 0.04$; Qwen3-32B 55.3\\%, $p = 0.076$; Likert n.s.).\n165\tGEE decomposition reveals the strongest originality confound ($\\text{OR} = 5.72$, $p < 0.0001$) alongside a marginally significant style effect ($\\text{OR} = 1.91$, $p = 0.046$; Table~\\ref{tab:gee}).\n166\tThis exploratory finding does not enter the primary Holm-Bonferroni family.\n167\t\n168\t\\subsection{GEE Conditional Decomposition and Confound Structure}\n169\t\\label{sec:direction}\n170\t\n171\tDirection analysis reveals partial confounding between style and original quality across axes; GEE conditional decomposition \\citep{liang1986gee} separates the two effects (full direction-asymmetry analysis in Appendix~\\ref{app:direction}).\n172\tAfter controlling for original preference, the formality effect remains significant for both judges (GPT-4o: $\\text{OR} = 2.58$, $p = 0.002$; Qwen3-32B: $\\text{OR} = 2.15$, $p < 0.0001$, $n = 355$; Table~\\ref{tab:gee}); the significant originality OR ($2.32$, $p = 0.006$) confirms residual $R \\to Q$ confounding, so this is a partial rather than clean separation.\n173\tA $4\\times$ scaled replication ($n = 355$) confirms the Blindspot: BT stable at $67.5\\%$ (halved CI), Likert still non-significant ($p = 0.60$); the OR decrease ($4.20 \\to 2.15$) reflects small-sample correction.\n174\t\n175\t\\begin{table}[t]\n176\t\\centering\n177\t\\small\n178\t\\caption{GEE conditional decomposition (exchangeable correlation) of style and originality effects. Verbosity shows no observed originality confound; formality and register show comparable style effects with varying originality confounds.}\n179\t\\label{tab:gee}\n180\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llclc@{}}\n181\t\\toprule\n182\t & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{Style OR} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{Originality OR} \\\\\n183\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \\cmidrule(lr){4-5}\n184\tConfig & OR & $p$ & OR & $p$ \\\\\n185\t\\midrule\n186\tQwen3 verb.\\ & 13.10 & ${<}.0001$ & 0.49 & .096 \\\\\n187\tGPT-4o verb.\\ & 11.34 & ${<}.0001$ & 0.45 & .062 \\\\\n188\tLlama-70B verb.\\ & 8.21 & ${<}.0001$ & 0.38 & .020 \\\\\n189\t\\midrule\n190\tGPT-4o form.\\ & 2.58 & .002 & 2.32 & .006 \\\\\n191\tQwen3 form.\\ & 4.20 & .0002 & 4.70 & .0001 \\\\\n192\tQwen3 form.$^a$ & 2.15 & ${<}.0001$ & 4.99 & ${<}.0001$ \\\\\n193\tLlama-70B form.\\ & 4.81 & ${<}.0001$ & 3.12 & .0007 \\\\\n194\t\\midrule\n195\tGPT-4o reg.\\ & 1.88 & .029 & 2.21 & .006 \\\\\n196\tQwen3 reg.\\ & 1.91 & .046 & 5.72 & ${<}.0001$ \\\\\n197\t\\bottomrule\n198\t\\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\footnotesize $^a$ $n = 355$; other rows: $n = 88$. 95\\% CIs in Appendix.}\n199\t\\end{tabular}\n200\t\\end{table}\n201\t\n202\tThe confound structure is consistent across all three target judges: verbosity is style-dominated (Style OR $= 8.2$--$13.1$, Orig OR ${<}1$); formality and register show comparable style effects (OR $\\approx 1.9$--$4.8$) alongside significant originality confounds.\n203\tCross-benchmark replication confirms formality bias across all six benchmark$\\times$judge configurations (BT 63--73\\%, all $p < 0.001$; AlpacaEval verbosity: BT 84.1\\%, Style OR $= 28.64$); Claude Sonnet 4 shows no style preference (BT 56.2\\%/54.1\\%, n.s.) but extreme originality sensitivity (Orig OR $= 6.66$, $p < 0.0001$).\n204\tThe Blindspot manifests in eight of eleven bias-positive cells; three exceptions (cross-benchmark BT $> 69\\%$, Likert $d \\geq 0.14$) confirm that only extreme effects cross Likert's threshold.\n205\tSame-style double-rewrite controls confirm a rewriting artifact: judges prefer LLM-polished text over originals (BT $P_{\\text{orig}}$: formality $.42/.38$; verbosity $.17/.08$; all $p < .04$; Appendix~\\ref{app:artifact_control}), validating GEE decomposition: the artifact cancels in GEE's originality term, leaving Style ORs uncontaminated.\n206\tDebiased prompting shows axis-dependent efficacy: verbosity bias is partially reducible (GPT-4o: $76.0\\% \\to 62.9\\%$, $-13.1$pp, $p = .0016$) while formality bias is nearly impervious (GPT-4o: $-0.4$pp; Qwen3-32B: $-5.7$pp).\n207\tThis asymmetry reinforces the per-axis confound structure: different axes require different intervention strategies.\n208\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01APExJ8cK9eGvZRntXSdyKN","session_id":"84c1e33b-a907-4d62-a6bc-d61bef62f953","uuid":"d3b2d7da-9aab-4d28-86e7-bb7970eef8c5","timestamp":"2026-05-21T16:12:39.755Z"}
