{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SJyuUeCMsBnhbbZ1H4cHNr","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260521T152517\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: 3.5 — Borderline Conference\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-21T16:10:09.941985+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# DREAM Review Report — \"The Likert Blindspot\"\n9\t\n10\t**Dream ID:** 20260521T152517 | **Venue:** EMNLP 2026 (ARR) | **Review Mode:** Full (2-Reviewer Roundtable)\n11\t\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t## Scores\n15\t\n16\t| Dimension | R1 (Methods) | R2 (Novelty) | Consensus |\n17\t|-----------|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n18\t| Soundness | 3.5 | 3.5 | **3.5** |\n19\t| Excitement | 3.5 | 3.5 | **3.5** |\n20\t| Reproducibility | 2.5 | 2.5 | **2.5** |\n21\t| **Overall Assessment** | 3.5 | 3.5 | **3.5** |\n22\t\n23\t**Overall Assessment: 3.5 — Borderline Conference** (strong Findings candidate; revisions could bring to 4.0 Main)\n24\t\n25\t---\n26\t\n27\t## Summary of Contributions\n28\t\n29\tThe paper introduces Controlled Style Decomposition (CSD), a black-box diagnostic pipeline for detecting per-axis style bias in LLM judges. It combines NLI-gated counterfactual generation, dual measurement protocol (Likert + pairwise), and GEE confound decomposition. Two main contributions:\n30\t\n31\t- **C1 (Likert Blindspot):** Pairwise BT detects style bias (63–77%) that 7 Likert methods miss (d→0), including a 100-point Likert ablation ruling out scale granularity.\n32\t- **C2 (Per-axis confound structure):** GEE reveals three regimes (pure/conditional/originality-dominated) replicating across 3 independent judge architectures.\n33\t\n34\t---\n35\t\n36\t## Strengths\n37\t\n38\t### S1. Well-Defined, Practically Important Finding\n39\tThe Likert Blindspot is operationally defined (BT sig AND all Likert NS), empirically demonstrated across 8/9 judge×axis cells, and has clear implications for evaluation practice. The finding that standard Likert auditing misses biases of BT 63–77% magnitude is directly actionable for the LLM evaluation community.\n40\t\n41\t### S2. 100-Point Likert Ablation (Strongest Novel Evidence)\n42\tThis is the paper's most compelling experiment. By showing that even 100 discrete scoring levels fail to detect bias that BT finds at 63–77%, it cleanly rules out the \"scale too coarse\" alternative explanation. The tie-rate reduction (58–73% → 28–35%) demonstrates the scale *did* increase variance, yet d remains negligible — a strong argument that measurement mode, not granularity, is the issue.\n43\t\n44\t### S3. Three-Architecture Replication with Identical Confound Structures\n45\tGPT-4o (OpenAI), Qwen3-32B (Alibaba), and Llama-3.3-70B (Meta) — three independently trained RLHF systems — produce near-identical bias profiles and confound regime assignments. This is strong evidence against architecture-specific artifacts and supports the \"RLHF training → style bias\" interpretation.\n46\t\n47\t### S4. Unusually Thorough Robustness Analysis (Five Controls)\n48\tThe five-layer defense against amplification is significantly more thorough than typical papers in this area:\n49\t1. NLI ablation reversal (formality BT flips from 66.5% → 41.7%) — a clever falsification test\n50\t2. Claude negative control (same pipeline, null result)\n51\t3. Placebo + GEE decomposition (honest artifact acknowledgment)\n52\t4. Cross-axis gradient (monotonic with manipulation invasiveness)\n53\t5. Three independent architectures converge\n54\t\n55\tThe NLI ablation reversal (§5.1) is particularly compelling: if pairwise format merely amplified bias, removing the NLI gate should amplify *more*, but instead the signal reverses. This is a strong anti-amplification argument.\n56\t\n57\t### S5. Per-Axis Confound Taxonomy (C2)\n58\tThe three-regime classification (pure/conditional/originality-dominated) is prescriptive and actionable. Verbosity can be taken at face value; formality requires GEE decomposition; register claims are not supported after decomposition. This differentiation is novel and practically useful.\n59\t\n60\t### S6. Honest Framing\n61\tThe paper acknowledges rewriting artifacts, maintains agnosticism on mechanism (attenuation vs. construct divergence), and limits claims to detection rather than asserting harm. This intellectual honesty strengthens credibility.\n62\t\n63\t---\n64\t\n65\t## Weaknesses\n66\t\n67\t### W1. Missing Power Analysis (Major — Both Reviewers)\n68\tThe central claim that \"all 7 Likert methods fail\" is weakened by the absence of a formal power analysis. At n=100:\n69\t- Power to detect d=0.02 (formality 100pt) ≈ 5% (essentially chance)\n70\t- Power to detect d=0.15 ≈ 30%\n71\t- Power to detect d=0.20 (small effect threshold) ≈ 50%\n72\t\n73\tLikert NS at n=100 is therefore *expected* for small effects, not evidence of a structural limitation. The paper partially addresses this with the scaled replication bootstrap curve (d flat at −0.017 across n=50→355), but this is shown for only one condition (Qwen3×LLMBar×formality). A reviewer hostile to C1 could argue: \"You simply didn't have enough power — show me the bootstrap curve for all 8 Blindspot cells.\"\n74\t\n75\t**Recommendation:** Add post-hoc power analysis. Show that even at n=355, Likert d remains negligible. Ideally compute the sample size needed for Likert to detect BT-equivalent effects (likely n > 10,000), which would strengthen the \"structural limitation\" interpretation.\n76\t\n77\t### W2. Very Sparse Related Work and References (Major — R2)\n78\tOnly **10 references** — far below the EMNLP norm of 30–50. Section 2 is ~200 words, covering only 3 topic clusters. Critical omissions:\n79\t- **Preference Leakage (ICLR'26):** Directly relevant — shows model-family bias mediated by style. Should be cited and differentiated.\n80\t- **Soumik et al. (2026):** Comprehensive debiasing comparison (style bias 0.76–0.92 >> position bias ≤0.04). Supports this paper's premise.\n81\t- **FairJudge (2026):** Training-side debiasing. Orthogonal but relevant.\n82\t- **Human evaluation methodology literature:** Belz et al. (2023), Howcroft et al. (2020) on Likert vs. ranking in NLG evaluation — directly relevant to the psychometric argument.\n83\t- **Thurstone (1927) and Stevens (1946):** Name-dropped but not formally cited in the reference list.\n84\t- **Position bias:** Only [6] cited; Wang et al. (2023), Ko et al. (2024) are standard.\n85\t\n86\t**Recommendation:** Expand to 30+ references. The Related Work section needs at least 2× expansion with proper positioning against Preference Leakage, Soumik, FairJudge, and the human evaluation methodology literature.\n87\t\n88\t### W3. No Figures (Major — R2)\n89\tThe paper contains **15 tables and 0 figures** across 8 pages. This is extremely unusual and hurts readability:\n90\t- No pipeline diagram for CSD\n91\t- No conceptual illustration of the Blindspot\n92\t- No forest plot for BT vs. Likert comparison\n93\t- No bootstrap attenuation curve visualization (described in text but not shown)\n94\t- No GEE regime visualization\n95\t\n96\tThe paper describes several figures in the review context (bootstrap curve, forest plots, regime heatmap) that would substantially improve communication. The data is rich enough to support compelling visualizations.\n97\t\n98\t**Recommendation:** Add at minimum: (1) CSD pipeline overview figure, (2) BT vs. Likert comparison forest plot or paired bar chart, (3) bootstrap attenuation curve. These could replace 2–3 less essential tables.\n99\t\n100\t### W4. Incomplete Experimental Coverage (Moderate — R1)\n101\tThe 3×3×3 matrix (judges × benchmarks × axes) is only partially filled:\n102\t- **Cross-benchmark (Table 8):** Only formality. Verbosity and register cross-benchmark data absent.\n103\t- **Register axis:** Only GPT-4o and Qwen3 on LLMBar. No Llama register.\n104\t- **Claude negative control:** Only formality×LLMBar. Would be more convincing with verbosity.\n105\t- **Rewriter independence (Table 12):** Only formality with GPT-4o as judge. Claims generalization to verbosity (\"Δ ≤ 3.8pp\") without showing the table.\n106\t- **Scaled replication:** Only Qwen3×LLMBar×formality.\n107\t\n108\tThe paper claims \"three benchmarks\" and \"three axes\" in the abstract but doesn't deliver the full matrix. The missing cells are particularly problematic for register (weakest axis) and cross-benchmark verbosity (strongest axis, untested on AlpacaEval/MT-Bench in the paper).\n109\t\n110\t**Recommendation:** Fill the most critical gaps: (1) Llama register, (2) verbosity cross-benchmark for at least one additional benchmark, (3) rewriter independence table for verbosity.\n111\t\n112\t### W5. GEE Model Specification (Moderate — R1)\n113\tEquation 1 uses only two covariates (S, O) with no interaction term. Several concerns:\n114\t- **Working correlation structure** not specified (exchangeable? independent?). This affects standard errors and significance.\n115\t- **No S×O interaction term.** For the \"conditional\" regime (formality), the interaction between style and originality is the core finding — yet the model doesn't include it.\n116\t- **Missing covariates:** Text length differences, benchmark difficulty, NLI confidence score could all confound.\n117\t- **GEE CI width:** For Llama formality, Style OR CI is [1.55, 6.2] — very wide. This precision issue is acknowledged in Limitations but not addressed.\n118\t\n119\t**Recommendation:** Report the correlation structure. Add the S×O interaction term and report whether it changes the regime classification. Discuss potential omitted variable bias.\n120\t\n121\t### W6. Reproducibility Gaps (Moderate — R1)\n122\tInsufficient implementation detail:\n123\t- NLI model identity not specified (DeBERTa-v3? RoBERTa-MNLI?)\n124\t- Rewriting prompts not provided\n125\t- Temperature/sampling parameters not given\n126\t- API versions and dates not specified\n127\t- No code or data availability statement\n128\t- Bootstrap CI type (percentile vs. BCa) not specified\n129\t\n130\t### W7. No Responsible NLP Research Checklist (Minor but Risk — R2)\n131\tARR requires the Responsible NLP Research Checklist (Sections A–E). It is not visible in this submission. Incomplete or missing checklist is flagged as a desk-reject signal in ARR guidelines.\n132\t\n133\t### W8. Multiple Comparisons Not Addressed (Minor — R1)\n134\tTable 1 tests 9 judge×axis cells. No Bonferroni/Holm correction is applied or discussed. While 7/8 significant cells have p < 0.01 (surviving any reasonable correction), the omission should be acknowledged.\n135\t\n136\t---\n137\t\n138\t## Detailed Assessment by Dimension\n139\t\n140\t### Soundness: 3.5/5\n141\tThe CSD pipeline is methodologically sound in concept: NLI gating, position balancing, BT modeling, and GEE decomposition are all appropriate choices. The five robustness controls are thorough. However, the power analysis gap (W1) undermines the central claim, the GEE specification is incomplete (W5), and the NLI model's own limitations (lexical overlap sensitivity, threshold effects) are not discussed.\n142\t\n143\t### Excitement: 3.5/5\n144\tThe Likert Blindspot finding is practically important and the 100-point ablation is genuinely novel in this setting. The per-axis confound taxonomy is a useful prescriptive contribution. However, the core finding (comparative > absolute for small differences) is well-established in psychometrics, and the novelty is primarily in the empirical demonstration, not in theoretical advance. The paper detects bias but proposes no debiasing — its scope is purely diagnostic.\n145\t\n146\t### Reproducibility: 2.5/5\n147\tThe pipeline is described at a level sufficient for conceptual understanding but not for replication. Missing: NLI model identity, prompts, temperature, API versions, code/data. The dual measurement protocol adds complexity that makes independent replication harder without these details.\n148\t\n149\t---\n150\t\n151\t## Comparison to Imagined Experiments\n152\t\n153\tThe review context lists 12 imagined experiments. Coverage assessment:\n154\t\n155\t| Experiment | In Paper? | Quality |\n156\t|-----------|:---------:|---------|\n157\t| exp-001 (Multi-judge Claude/Gemini) | Partial — Claude done, Gemini absent | Claude negative control well-used |\n158\t| exp-002 (Multi-benchmark) | Partial — formality only | Table 8 covers 3×3 for formality |\n159\t| exp-003 (7-method sensitivity) | Yes — Llama, claims replication | Table 2 is convincing |\n160\t| exp-005 (Placebo control) | Yes — 3 judges | Table 11 is thorough |\n161\t| exp-006 (Scaled n=355) | Yes — single condition | Table 13; bootstrap curve described not shown |\n162\t| exp-009 (Rewriter independence) | Yes — limited scope | Table 12; only formality×GPT-4o judge |\n163\t| exp-020 (GPT-4o full axis) | Yes | Table 9 |\n164\t| exp-021 (Llama third judge) | Yes | Tables 6, 7 |\n165\t| exp-022 (Double-rewrite placebo) | Yes — merged with exp-005 | Covered in Table 11 |\n166\t| exp-023 (NLI gate ablation) | Yes | Table 10 — compelling |\n167\t| exp-024 (Cross-axis gradient) | Yes | Table 14 — qualitative only |\n168\t| 100-point Likert ablation | Yes | Tables 3, 4 — strongest new evidence |\n169\t\n170\t**Coverage: ~10/12 experiments present**, with most gaps being scope limitations (cross-benchmark only for formality, rewriter independence narrow). The 100-point Likert ablation and NLI ablation reversal are the strongest experiments and both are well-presented.\n171\t\n172\t---\n173\t\n174\t## Questions for Authors\n175\t\n176\t1. **Power:** What sample size would Likert need to detect the BT-equivalent effect? If the answer is n > 5,000, this strengthens C1 enormously. If n ~ 500 would suffice, it weakens it.\n177\t2. **GEE interaction:** Does adding S×O to Eq. 1 change the regime classification for any judge×axis cell?\n178\t3. **NLI threshold:** How sensitive are results to NLI confidence threshold? What happens at 0.8 vs. 0.9 entailment probability?\n179\t4. **Bootstrap curve:** Can you show the attenuation curve (Likert d vs. n) for verbosity and a second judge?\n180\t5. **Register completeness:** Why is Llama×register absent? Is it a resource limitation or did it fail quality checks?\n181\t\n182\t---\n183\t\n184\t## Verdict and Recommendation\n185\t\n186\t**Overall: 3.5 — Borderline Conference / Strong Findings**\n187\t\n188\tThis paper addresses a genuinely important question (can Likert auditing detect style bias?) with a well-designed pipeline and thorough robustness analysis. The core finding is convincing and practically important. The 100-point Likert ablation is the strongest single piece of evidence and is novel in this setting. The three-architecture replication and five-layer defense are unusually thorough.\n189\t\n190\tHowever, the paper is held back by: (1) a missing power analysis that weakens the central claim, (2) very sparse related work and references (10 refs vs. 30+ norm), (3) no figures despite data-rich content, (4) incomplete experimental coverage, and (5) reproducibility gaps.\n191\t\n192\t**Path to 4.0 (Main Conference):**\n193\t1. Add formal power analysis showing Likert would need n >> 1,000 to detect BT-equivalent effects\n194\t2. Expand Related Work to 30+ references with proper positioning against Preference Leakage, Soumik, FairJudge\n195\t3. Add 3–4 figures (pipeline, BT vs Likert comparison, bootstrap curve, regime visualization)\n196\t4. Fill coverage gaps: Llama register, verbosity cross-benchmark, Claude on verbosity\n197\t5. Report GEE correlation structure, add S×O interaction, discuss omitted variables\n198\t6. Provide prompts, NLI model identity, and code/data availability statement\n199\t7. Include Responsible NLP Research Checklist\n200\t\n201\tThe contribution is real and the experimental design is sound at its core — the issues are primarily in completeness and presentation. A focused revision could bring this to a competitive Main conference submission.\n202\t\n203\t---\n204\t\n205\t*Review conducted by 2-reviewer roundtable: R1 (Methods & Experiments), R2 (Novelty & Related Work). Full 4-step protocol: independent → cross-share → converge → synthesize.*"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01APExJ8cK9eGvZRntXSdyKN","session_id":"84c1e33b-a907-4d62-a6bc-d61bef62f953","uuid":"1e07ffcc-0e35-4d2f-a0c9-4849450ce5f5","timestamp":"2026-05-21T16:13:08.522Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"ab7c13a0302c8354d","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Wj2CiaHKUBUwsCGmV3oRYB","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":79027,"tool_uses":20,"duration_ms":178417},"uuid":"482d4526-aeb1-4a20-928e-aca2a7a4715a","session_id":"84c1e33b-a907-4d62-a6bc-d61bef62f953"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Wj2CiaHKUBUwsCGmV3oRYB","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper, methodology, experiments, code, and project context. Let me produce the review.\n\n## R1: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary of Assessment\n\nThis paper presents CSD, a well-engineered diagnostic pipeline for detecting per-axis style bias in LLM judges, with the central finding that Likert scoring systematically misses biases detected by pairwise comparison (the \"Likert Blindspot\"). The methodology is generally sound and the experimental coverage is commendable given the paper's scope. However, several concerns around statistical rigor (small denominators for headline claims, limited multiple-comparison correction clarity, single-benchmark primary design) and the fundamental difficulty of separating detection from amplification prevent a fully confident endorsement of all claims at the strength they are stated.\n\n### Methodology\n\n**CSD pipeline design.** The three-stage pipeline (style rewriting, NLI gating, dual measurement) is well-motivated and logically structured. The idea of combining interventional text manipulation with content-preservation filtering is a genuine methodological advance over purely correlational approaches. The paper correctly positions itself as providing \"interventional rather than formal causal evidence\" (Section 3.5), which is an appropriately honest framing.\n\n**GEE model specification.** The GEE logistic model with exchangeable working correlation is a reasonable choice for handling within-pair dependence from the A/B swap design. The paper addresses correlation structure robustness (Appendix C, showing exchangeable vs. independence yield virtually identical results with |delta QIC| < 0.001) and tests the interaction term (style x originality: non-significant for both judges). These diagnostics are above-average for this area.\n\nHowever, I have concerns about the GEE specification:\n- The model includes only two binary predictors (style position and originality). Other potential confounders such as text length, task type, or difficulty are not modeled. For verbosity, where length is the manipulation itself, this is acknowledged (length ratio OR = 1.03, NS), but for formality, task-type heterogeneity is unexamined.\n- Random effects models (GLMM) would be a natural alternative that can capture item-level heterogeneity. The paper does not discuss why GEE was preferred over GLMM. GEE provides population-averaged estimates, which are appropriate for the stated claims, but the choice should be justified.\n- The intercept in the GEE captures position bias, but the paper does not report its magnitude or significance. Since position bias is a known confounder in LLM evaluation, this should be explicitly discussed.\n\n**NLI gating.** This is the most critical methodological component, and its validity directly determines whether the measured effects reflect style bias or content confounds. The approach has clear strengths: bidirectional entailment with a high threshold (0.90), direction-aware gating for verbosity (a thoughtful innovation), and extensive validation (11-threshold sweep, LLM annotator cross-check, GPT-4o full-scale validation).\n\nFailure modes to consider:\n- The NLI model (DeBERTa-v3-large) has a known blind spot for paraphrastic divergence: two texts can be mutually entailing while differing in perceived informativeness, coherence, or emphasis. The paper acknowledges this in the Limitations (\"cannot block quality perception shifts mediated by style itself\") but does not quantify the risk.\n- The direction-aware gate for verbosity (only backward entailment for expansions) is permissive by design: GPT-4o validation shows 17.4% false positives for verbosity vs. 7.9% for formality. This means roughly 1 in 6 verbosity pairs may have content differences that slipped through, yet verbosity is presented as the \"cleanest\" axis. The paper frames estimates as \"lower bounds,\" but this is only valid if content contamination uniformly biases toward the null, which is not established.\n- The NLI gate uses a single model (DeBERTa-v3-large). While the GPT-4o cross-validation provides some comfort, both systems share similar training distributions and may share systematic blind spots.\n\n**Bradley-Terry model.** Using BT to aggregate pairwise comparisons is standard and appropriate. The implementation uses the choix library with spectral initialization and MM estimation. The goodness-of-fit tests (Appendix F) show adequate fit. One concern: BT assumes transitivity and a single latent quality dimension. If judges respond to style on a different dimension than quality, BT may conflate these. The paper uses BT only for binary style preference (styled vs. unstyled), so this concern is somewhat mitigated.\n\n**Position control.** Presenting each pair in both orders and using all observations (including inconsistent ones) for BT is appropriate. The consistency filter (56.1% retention) discards nearly half the data for the sign test; the paper correctly notes that both approaches yield convergent results. Position bias statistics are reported (Trial 1 A-rate vs. Trial 2 A-rate), though the actual position bias magnitude is not prominently discussed.\n\n### Experimental Design\n\n**Sample sizes.** The primary analyses use LLMBar with n=100 pairs, yielding n=86-88 after NLI filtering. After position doubling, BT uses ~173 observations per cell, which is adequate for the observed effect sizes (BT 63-77%). However:\n- For GEE decomposition with two predictors on ~176 observations (88 pairs x 2 trials), the model is feasible but effect estimates have wide confidence intervals (e.g., Style OR CI for Qwen3 verbosity is [5.62, 30.55]). Claims about the *magnitude* of style vs. originality effects should be interpreted cautiously.\n- The sign test uses only consistent pairs (n~46-55), which provides low precision for subgroup analyses.\n- The scaled replication (n=355) is conducted for only one judge x one axis (Qwen3-32B formality). While this produces the smoking-gun evidence for measurement attenuation (d converges to 0), the absence of scaled replication for other cells means we cannot distinguish power limitation from measurement attenuation across the full matrix.\n\n**Statistical methodology.** The bootstrap BT (10K pair-level resamples) is appropriate and conservative. Paired t-tests for Likert ATE are standard. Robust sandwich SEs for GEE are correct for the clustered design. The seven-method sensitivity hierarchy (Table 2) is a compelling approach to demonstrating that the Likert Blindspot is method-independent rather than test-specific.\n\n**Multiple comparisons.** The paper designates a primary family of eight tests and applies Holm-Bonferroni correction. This is clearly stated and appropriate. However, the boundary between the primary family and \"exploratory\" analyses is somewhat convenient: register analyses, which show weaker effects, are classified as exploratory and thus escape correction. The cross-benchmark analyses (Table 1, AlpacaEval and MT-Bench rows) appear to be tested at nominal alpha without clear family-wise correction across benchmarks. This is acknowledged implicitly (\"cross-benchmark validation focuses on strongest-evidence configurations\") but deserves explicit discussion.\n\n**The \"90% Blindspot rate\" claim.** This headline statistic (9/10 BT-significant cells show Likert NS) has a denominator of only 10. With such a small denominator, the 90% figure is fragile: if one additional cell had shown Likert significance, it would drop to 80%; if one BT-significant cell had been NS, the denominator changes. The paper would be more honest to frame this as \"the Blindspot manifests across the majority of tested configurations\" rather than attaching a specific percentage to 10 observations. The three exceptions (MT-Bench formality for both judges, AlpacaEval verbosity for Qwen3) are informatively discussed as confirming a severity spectrum, which partially addresses this.\n\n**Scaled replication scope.** The n=355 scaled replication is the strongest single piece of evidence in the paper, but it covers only Qwen3-32B x formality. The claim \"d converges to 0\" is established for this one cell. Verbosity shows d~0.15-0.19 at n=86, which could be either a small true effect attenuated by Likert or genuine measurement attenuation at a larger true effect. Without scaled replication for verbosity, the paper cannot definitively classify this cell. The bootstrap attenuation analysis (Appendix J) partially addresses this by showing d remains flat, but this is constrained by the original sample size.\n\n### Controls\n\n**1. Placebo control (double-rewrite).** Same-style double-rewrite shows judges prefer LLM-polished text (BT P(orig) = 0.08-0.42 across axes). This is an important finding that confirms a rewriting artifact exists. The paper argues GEE absorbs this artifact via the originality OR. This is mechanistically plausible: the originality predictor captures \"is original in preferred position,\" and the rewriting artifact would inflate this term. However, for verbosity, the originality OR is NS (0.49, p=0.096 for Qwen3), which means the placebo artifact (BT P(orig) = 0.08, p<0.0001 for Qwen3 verbosity) is *not* captured by the GEE model. This is a tension: a massive rewriting artifact exists (judges almost never prefer the original in verbosity), yet GEE says originality is non-significant. The resolution is that in the style-manipulation pairs (not the placebo pairs), the verbosity rewrite direction is bidirectional (verbose-to-concise and concise-to-verbose), and the style effect overwhelms the artifact. But this deserves more explicit discussion.\n\n**2. NLI gate ablation.** The reversal for formality NLI-fail pairs (BT 41.7%) is compelling evidence that the gate removes content contamination rather than amplifying signal. For verbosity, NLI-fail pairs remain at 75.0%, consistent with the dominant style effect. The formality reversal, however, is based on ~12 pairs, limiting precision.\n\n**3. Cross-axis gradient.** Three data points (register 55% < formality 67% < verbosity 77%) are presented as evidence against uniform format amplification. With only three points, this is qualitative at best. The paper appropriately labels this as a \"bounding analysis,\" but the cross-axis gradient is used more assertively in the anti-amplification argument (Section 4.4, item iii) than three data points warrant.\n\n**4. Claude negative control.** Claude Sonnet 4 showing no style bias (BT 56.2%/54.1%, NS) is useful discriminative evidence. One negative control is minimally sufficient; the ideal would be 2-3 judges showing no bias, or a judge known to be debiased. The TOST equivalence testing (Appendix E) bounds Claude's effect to <15pp, which is informative but not tight.\n\n**5. Rewriter independence.** GPT-4o as alternative rewriter produces statistically indistinguishable results from Qwen3-32B rewriter (formality: 64.4% vs. 66.5%; verbosity: 73.5% vs. 77.3%). This is a strong control for evaluator-producer bias, though the 22% decrease in Style OR for verbosity (13.10 to 10.17) suggests some evaluator-producer inflation. The paper acknowledges this.\n\n**Overall controls assessment.** The five-layer control structure is above-average for this literature. No single control is individually decisive, but the convergence of evidence is reasonably convincing that the detected effects are not pure artifacts. The weakest link is the format amplification argument: the Comparative Trap concern (Jeong et al. 2025) is real, and while the controls bound the risk, they do not eliminate it. The paper's dual-interpretation framing (measurement attenuation vs. construct divergence) is an honest way to handle this uncertainty.\n\n### Reproducibility\n\n**Experimental details.** The paper provides sufficient detail for reproduction: model identifiers (gpt-4o-2024-08-06), NLI model (DeBERTa-v3-large), temperature settings (0.7 for rewriting, 0.0 for judging), bootstrap parameters (10K resamples, seed 42), and NLI threshold (0.90). The rewriting prompts are detailed in the code but not fully reproduced in the paper; a supplementary appendix with exact prompts would help.\n\n**API-based judges.** GPT-4o and Claude Sonnet 4 are API models that may change behavior with version updates. The paper specifies the GPT-4o version (2024-08-06), which is good practice. Claude's version is identified as \"Claude Sonnet 4\" but without a specific snapshot date. Results may not replicate on future model versions.\n\n**Code and data.** \"Code and data will be released upon acceptance.\" This is standard but limits pre-publication verification. The codebase (from my inspection) is well-structured with separate modules for each pipeline stage.\n\n**Statistical software.** The paper uses the choix library for BT, statsmodels for GEE, and DeBERTa-v3-large for NLI. These are all publicly available. The BT regularization uses choix defaults, which should be stated explicitly (it uses a default regularization parameter that affects estimates when sample sizes are small).\n\n### Strengths (Methods/Experiments)\n\n1. **Comprehensive experimental matrix.** 4 judges x 3 benchmarks x 3 axes with dual measurement (Likert + pairwise) on identical data is a large and well-organized experimental design. The 12 tables covering distinct analyses show thoroughness.\n\n2. **Seven-method sensitivity hierarchy.** Testing the Blindspot with seven statistical methods (Table 2 and Appendix K) makes a convincing case that the finding is not an artifact of a particular statistical test. This is an unusually rigorous approach for the style-bias literature.\n\n3. **Honest epistemic framing.** The dual-interpretation approach (measurement attenuation vs. construct divergence) with the conclusion that both yield the same operational implication is scientifically mature. The Limitations section is substantive and identifies real weaknesses rather than token caveats.\n\n4. **GEE decomposition revealing per-axis confound structure.** The finding that verbosity is purely stylistic while formality is conditional and register is originality-dominated is a genuinely novel contribution that goes beyond simply detecting bias. The diagnostics (correlation structure comparison, interaction test) are thorough.\n\n5. **Direction-aware NLI gating.** The adaptation of the NLI gate for axes that inherently change information density (verbosity) is a methodological innovation that other researchers can adopt.\n\n### Weaknesses (Methods/Experiments)\n\n1. **[Moderate] Small primary dataset and limited scaled replication.** LLMBar has only 100 pairs, and the scaled replication (n=355) covers only one cell (Qwen3-32B x formality). The paper makes strong claims about a \"severity spectrum\" based on a handful of cells at different effect sizes, but these cells differ in benchmark, judge, and axis simultaneously, making it difficult to isolate what drives the spectrum. A controlled experiment varying only effect size (e.g., by titrating style intensity) would be needed to establish a true dose-response relationship.\n\n2. **[Moderate] The rewriting artifact threatens the pure-style claim for verbosity.** The placebo control shows verbosity has the most extreme rewriting artifact (Qwen3 BT P(orig) = 0.082), yet verbosity is presented as the \"cleanest\" axis with \"no observed originality confound.\" The GEE Originality OR being NS for verbosity may reflect the specific functional form of the model rather than the absence of artifact confounding. The paper's claim that \"the full 77.3% effect is attributable to style rather than originality\" is overstated given the placebo evidence. The convergent directional evidence (both directions favor verbose) is more convincing than the GEE NS result.\n\n3. **[Moderate] Multiple-comparison correction is incomplete across the full claim set.** The primary family (8 tests) is corrected, but the paper makes many additional claims: cross-benchmark replication, NLI ablation reversal, cross-axis gradient, debiased prompting effects, 100-point Likert NS, Claude equivalence. These are classified as \"exploratory\" or \"confirmatory\" with varying correction standards. While individual tests are valid, the cumulative probability of at least one false positive across the full analysis set is non-trivial.\n\n4. **[Minor] The \"90% Blindspot rate\" is fragile.** A denominator of 10 does not support a stable percentage. The three exceptions all involve stronger effects (BT > 69%), which actually undermines the Blindspot as a universal phenomenon and reframes it as a power-contingent effect. The paper acknowledges this (\"severity spectrum\") but the abstract and introduction lead with the 90% figure.\n\n5. **[Minor] No human evaluation of rewriting quality.** The NLI gate and LLM annotator validation are both automated. Human evaluation of a sample of rewritten texts would directly address the concern that NLI-passing pairs still differ in perceived quality. The Limitations section acknowledges this, but given how central content preservation is to the entire framework, even a small-scale human evaluation (50 pairs) would substantially strengthen the paper.\n\n### Missing Experiments\n\n1. **Scaled replication for verbosity.** The n=355 formality replication is the paper's strongest evidence, but verbosity shows d~0.15-0.19 at n=86 (borderline non-zero). A scaled verbosity replication would determine whether this represents genuine partial detection or measurement attenuation at a different point on the severity spectrum.\n\n2. **Human evaluation of rewriting quality.** A small-scale human study (e.g., 50 pairs x 2 annotators) rating content preservation would directly validate the NLI gate and address the most fundamental threat to validity.\n\n3. **Effect size titration.** The severity spectrum claim (complete Blindspot at moderate bias, partial at strong) would be more convincing with controlled variation of style intensity (e.g., parameterized rewriting prompts producing weak, moderate, and strong style shifts) on a single judge x benchmark.\n\n4. **Formal equivalence testing (TOST) for Likert.** The paper mentions this in Limitations but does not execute it for the main Blindspot cells. Demonstrating that the Likert effect is not just non-significant but formally equivalent to zero (TOST at a meaningful delta) would strengthen the measurement attenuation interpretation.\n\n5. **Position bias magnitude.** The GEE intercept captures position bias but its magnitude and significance are not reported. Given the extensive literature on position bias in LLM evaluation, this should be explicitly quantified.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. The placebo control shows massive rewriting artifacts for verbosity (P(orig) = 0.08), yet the GEE Originality OR for verbosity is NS. How do you reconcile these? Is it possible that the bidirectional design (verbose-to-concise + concise-to-verbose) masks the artifact rather than controls for it?\n\n2. The NLI gate uses direction-aware (unidirectional) verification for verbosity, which admits 17.4% false positives per GPT-4o validation. Have you assessed whether these false-positive pairs show systematically different BT estimates than true-positive pairs?\n\n3. For the GEE model: what is the estimated position bias (intercept term), and is it significant? Do results change if you add task-type fixed effects or random item intercepts?\n\n4. The 100-point Likert still shows 52-66% tie rates. Have you examined whether these ties reflect genuine indifference (the judge truly rates both texts identically) or a different mechanism (e.g., the judge defaulting to a round number)?\n\n5. The paper tests debiased prompting but only for the pairwise mode. What happens to the Likert Blindspot under debiased prompting --- does it narrow, widen, or remain unchanged?\n\n### Preliminary Scores\n\n- **Soundness:** 3.5/5. The pipeline is well-designed and the core finding (pairwise detects what Likert misses) is convincingly demonstrated. The GEE decomposition is a genuine contribution. Deductions for: incomplete reconciliation of placebo artifacts with pure-style claims, limited scaled replication, and the inherent difficulty of separating detection from amplification (though the controls help).\n\n- **Reproducibility:** 3.5/5. Sufficient detail for most analyses; specific model versions given for GPT-4o. Deductions for: code/data not yet released, exact rewriting prompts not in the paper, API model ephemerality, and choix library defaults not explicitly stated.\n\n- **Overall lean:** 3.5 (Borderline Conference). The paper addresses a timely and important problem (measurement methodology for LLM judge bias), introduces a well-engineered pipeline with genuine methodological innovations (direction-aware NLI gating, GEE per-axis decomposition), and the Likert Blindspot finding has clear practical implications. The experimental coverage is broad and the five-layer control structure is commendable. However, the primary dataset is small (n=100), scaled replication is limited to one cell, the format amplification concern is bounded but not eliminated, and the \"90% Blindspot rate\" overstates the strength of evidence from 10 cells. If the authors address the placebo-vs-GEE tension for verbosity and provide scaled replication for at least one additional axis, this would strengthen toward a 4.0. As submitted, the honest epistemic framing and methodological contributions place it above typical Findings-level work, but the residual concerns about whether pairwise is detecting or amplifying bias, combined with the limited dataset scale, keep it at the Conference borderline. Calibrating to EMNLP 2026 acceptance rates (~21% Main, Findings available at 3.0), this paper is competitive for Main track but would benefit from one round of targeted revision."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ab7c13a0302c8354d (use SendMessage with to: 'ab7c13a0302c8354d' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 83677\ntool_uses: 20\nduration_ms: 178419</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"84c1e33b-a907-4d62-a6bc-d61bef62f953","uuid":"56833540-f10e-43ca-9183-0d28656d6ee4","timestamp":"2026-05-21T16:14:32.943Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus: **Methods soundness and Experimental rigor**.\n\n## Venue Calibration (EMNLP 2026 ARR)\n- 5 = Award consideration (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference-worthy (Main track, ~21% acceptance)\n- 3 = Findings-worthy\n- 2 = Major revision needed\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\nDimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5)\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition\"\n\nThe paper introduces Controlled Style Decomposition (CSD), a black-box diagnostic pipeline for detecting per-axis style bias in LLM judges. CSD combines:\n1. Per-axis style manipulation via LLM rewriter (Qwen3-32B default, GPT-4o alternative)\n2. NLI-gated content control (GPT-4o NLI model filters content-altered pairs)\n3. Position-controlled pairwise comparison (A/B swap) with Bradley-Terry preference + Likert scoring on identical stimuli\n4. GEE conditional decomposition: logit(P[prefer r'_a]) = β₀ + β_style·1[style shift] + β_orig·1[rewritten], yielding Style OR and Originality OR\n\n**Key findings**:\n- (C2a) Likert Blindspot: 9/10 BT-significant cells show Likert NS — 90% blindspot rate. Scaled replication (n=100→355) confirms d≈0 is structural, not power gap.\n- (C2b) Per-axis confound structure: verbosity = pure style (Style OR 5.48–13.10, Orig OR NS), formality = conditional (both ORs sig for GPT-4o/Qwen3), register = originality-dominated (Orig OR > Style OR)\n- (C1) CSD pipeline validated on 4 judges × 3 benchmarks × 3 axes\n- (C3) Architecture-dependent susceptibility: verbosity 3/3 target judges, formality 2/3\n\n**Experiments**:\n- 4 judges: GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B, Llama-3.3-70B, Claude Sonnet 4 (negative control)\n- 3 benchmarks: LLMBar (primary, 100 pairs), AlpacaEval, MT-Bench\n- 3 style axes: formality, verbosity, register\n- 12 tables covering: sensitivity comparison (Table 1), GEE decomposition (Table 2), GPT-4o verbosity/register (Table 3), formality across judges (Table 4), evidence matrix (Table 5), Llama-70B profile (Table 6), cross-benchmark formality (Table 7), scaled replication (Table 8), rewriter independence (Table 9), placebo control (Table 10), NLI ablation (Table 11), cross-axis gradient (Table 12)\n\n**Robustness controls (5-layer)**:\n1. Placebo control: original vs double-rewrite — rewriting artifact confirmed, GEE Orig OR models it\n2. NLI gate ablation: removing NLI gate *reverses* formality BT (41.7%), disconfirming amplification\n3. Cross-axis gradient: NLI rejection rate positively correlates with BT magnitude (3 data points, qualitative)\n4. Negative control: Claude Sonnet shows no significant bias on either axis\n5. Rewriter independence: GPT-4o rewriter produces statistically indistinguishable results from Qwen3 rewriter\n\n**Imagined experiment coverage**: The paper covers all 11 imagined experiments from the design document (multi-judge formality, multi-benchmark, Likert-vs-pairwise sensitivity, placebo control, scaled replication, non-Qwen rewriter, GPT-4o verbosity/register, Llama-70B third judge, NLI ablation, cross-axis gradient). Coverage is complete.\n\n## Your Task\nProduce a detailed review addressing:\n\n1. **Methodology Assessment**\n   - Is the CSD pipeline well-motivated and sound?\n   - Is the GEE model specification appropriate? Are there alternative specifications that should be considered (e.g., interaction terms, random effects)?\n   - Is the NLI gating approach a valid content control? What are its failure modes?\n   - Is the Bradley-Terry model appropriate for this setting?\n   - Position control (A/B swap) — is this sufficient?\n\n2. **Experimental Design**\n   - Sample sizes: LLMBar has 100 pairs — after NLI filtering, n≈86-88. Is this sufficient for the claims made?\n   - Statistical methodology: bootstrap BT (10K iterations), paired t-tests for Likert, robust sandwich SEs for GEE — appropriate?\n   - Multiple comparisons: the paper tests many judge×benchmark×axis cells. Is there correction for multiple testing?\n   - The \"90% blindspot rate\" denominator is only 10 cells. Is this a robust statistic?\n   - Scaled replication goes from n=100 to n=355 for one judge×axis only. Is broader replication needed?\n\n3. **Controls Assessment**\n   - Evaluate each of the 5 anti-amplification controls. Are they convincing?\n   - The cross-axis gradient has only 3 data points — is this meaningful evidence?\n   - Claude as negative control — is one negative control sufficient?\n   - Placebo control shows all judges prefer rewritten text — does this threaten the pure style claim for verbosity?\n\n4. **Reproducibility**\n   - Are the experimental details sufficient for reproduction?\n   - API-based judges (GPT-4o, Claude) may change over time\n   - Is the code/data availability mentioned?\n\n5. **Weaknesses**\n   - Identify the top 3-5 weaknesses in methods and experiments\n   - For each, assess severity (minor/moderate/major)\n\n6. **Missing Experiments or Analyses**\n   - What additional experiments would strengthen the paper?\n   - Any ablations that are missing?\n\nOutput format:\n```\n## R1: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary of Assessment\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Methodology\n[detailed assessment]\n\n### Experimental Design\n[detailed assessment]\n\n### Controls\n[detailed assessment]\n\n### Reproducibility\n[assessment]\n\n### Strengths (Methods/Experiments)\n1. ...\n2. ...\n3. ...\n\n### Weaknesses (Methods/Experiments)\n1. [severity] ...\n2. [severity] ...\n3. ...\n\n### Missing Experiments\n1. ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\n1. ...\n\n### Preliminary Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall lean: X.X (with brief justification calibrated to EMNLP 2026 acceptance rates)\n```\n","agentId":"ab7c13a0302c8354d","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper, methodology, experiments, code, and project context. Let me produce the review.\n\n## R1: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary of Assessment\n\nThis paper presents CSD, a well-engineered diagnostic pipeline for detecting per-axis style bias in LLM judges, with the central finding that Likert scoring systematically misses biases detected by pairwise comparison (the \"Likert Blindspot\"). The methodology is generally sound and the experimental coverage is commendable given the paper's scope. However, several concerns around statistical rigor (small denominators for headline claims, limited multiple-comparison correction clarity, single-benchmark primary design) and the fundamental difficulty of separating detection from amplification prevent a fully confident endorsement of all claims at the strength they are stated.\n\n### Methodology\n\n**CSD pipeline design.** The three-stage pipeline (style rewriting, NLI gating, dual measurement) is well-motivated and logically structured. The idea of combining interventional text manipulation with content-preservation filtering is a genuine methodological advance over purely correlational approaches. The paper correctly positions itself as providing \"interventional rather than formal causal evidence\" (Section 3.5), which is an appropriately honest framing.\n\n**GEE model specification.** The GEE logistic model with exchangeable working correlation is a reasonable choice for handling within-pair dependence from the A/B swap design. The paper addresses correlation structure robustness (Appendix C, showing exchangeable vs. independence yield virtually identical results with |delta QIC| < 0.001) and tests the interaction term (style x originality: non-significant for both judges). These diagnostics are above-average for this area.\n\nHowever, I have concerns about the GEE specification:\n- The model includes only two binary predictors (style position and originality). Other potential confounders such as text length, task type, or difficulty are not modeled. For verbosity, where length is the manipulation itself, this is acknowledged (length ratio OR = 1.03, NS), but for formality, task-type heterogeneity is unexamined.\n- Random effects models (GLMM) would be a natural alternative that can capture item-level heterogeneity. The paper does not discuss why GEE was preferred over GLMM. GEE provides population-averaged estimates, which are appropriate for the stated claims, but the choice should be justified.\n- The intercept in the GEE captures position bias, but the paper does not report its magnitude or significance. Since position bias is a known confounder in LLM evaluation, this should be explicitly discussed.\n\n**NLI gating.** This is the most critical methodological component, and its validity directly determines whether the measured effects reflect style bias or content confounds. The approach has clear strengths: bidirectional entailment with a high threshold (0.90), direction-aware gating for verbosity (a thoughtful innovation), and extensive validation (11-threshold sweep, LLM annotator cross-check, GPT-4o full-scale validation).\n\nFailure modes to consider:\n- The NLI model (DeBERTa-v3-large) has a known blind spot for paraphrastic divergence: two texts can be mutually entailing while differing in perceived informativeness, coherence, or emphasis. The paper acknowledges this in the Limitations (\"cannot block quality perception shifts mediated by style itself\") but does not quantify the risk.\n- The direction-aware gate for verbosity (only backward entailment for expansions) is permissive by design: GPT-4o validation shows 17.4% false positives for verbosity vs. 7.9% for formality. This means roughly 1 in 6 verbosity pairs may have content differences that slipped through, yet verbosity is presented as the \"cleanest\" axis. The paper frames estimates as \"lower bounds,\" but this is only valid if content contamination uniformly biases toward the null, which is not established.\n- The NLI gate uses a single model (DeBERTa-v3-large). While the GPT-4o cross-validation provides some comfort, both systems share similar training distributions and may share systematic blind spots.\n\n**Bradley-Terry model.** Using BT to aggregate pairwise comparisons is standard and appropriate. The implementation uses the choix library with spectral initialization and MM estimation. The goodness-of-fit tests (Appendix F) show adequate fit. One concern: BT assumes transitivity and a single latent quality dimension. If judges respond to style on a different dimension than quality, BT may conflate these. The paper uses BT only for binary style preference (styled vs. unstyled), so this concern is somewhat mitigated.\n\n**Position control.** Presenting each pair in both orders and using all observations (including inconsistent ones) for BT is appropriate. The consistency filter (56.1% retention) discards nearly half the data for the sign test; the paper correctly notes that both approaches yield convergent results. Position bias statistics are reported (Trial 1 A-rate vs. Trial 2 A-rate), though the actual position bias magnitude is not prominently discussed.\n\n### Experimental Design\n\n**Sample sizes.** The primary analyses use LLMBar with n=100 pairs, yielding n=86-88 after NLI filtering. After position doubling, BT uses ~173 observations per cell, which is adequate for the observed effect sizes (BT 63-77%). However:\n- For GEE decomposition with two predictors on ~176 observations (88 pairs x 2 trials), the model is feasible but effect estimates have wide confidence intervals (e.g., Style OR CI for Qwen3 verbosity is [5.62, 30.55]). Claims about the *magnitude* of style vs. originality effects should be interpreted cautiously.\n- The sign test uses only consistent pairs (n~46-55), which provides low precision for subgroup analyses.\n- The scaled replication (n=355) is conducted for only one judge x one axis (Qwen3-32B formality). While this produces the smoking-gun evidence for measurement attenuation (d converges to 0), the absence of scaled replication for other cells means we cannot distinguish power limitation from measurement attenuation across the full matrix.\n\n**Statistical methodology.** The bootstrap BT (10K pair-level resamples) is appropriate and conservative. Paired t-tests for Likert ATE are standard. Robust sandwich SEs for GEE are correct for the clustered design. The seven-method sensitivity hierarchy (Table 2) is a compelling approach to demonstrating that the Likert Blindspot is method-independent rather than test-specific.\n\n**Multiple comparisons.** The paper designates a primary family of eight tests and applies Holm-Bonferroni correction. This is clearly stated and appropriate. However, the boundary between the primary family and \"exploratory\" analyses is somewhat convenient: register analyses, which show weaker effects, are classified as exploratory and thus escape correction. The cross-benchmark analyses (Table 1, AlpacaEval and MT-Bench rows) appear to be tested at nominal alpha without clear family-wise correction across benchmarks. This is acknowledged implicitly (\"cross-benchmark validation focuses on strongest-evidence configurations\") but deserves explicit discussion.\n\n**The \"90% Blindspot rate\" claim.** This headline statistic (9/10 BT-significant cells show Likert NS) has a denominator of only 10. With such a small denominator, the 90% figure is fragile: if one additional cell had shown Likert significance, it would drop to 80%; if one BT-significant cell had been NS, the denominator changes. The paper would be more honest to frame this as \"the Blindspot manifests across the majority of tested configurations\" rather than attaching a specific percentage to 10 observations. The three exceptions (MT-Bench formality for both judges, AlpacaEval verbosity for Qwen3) are informatively discussed as confirming a severity spectrum, which partially addresses this.\n\n**Scaled replication scope.** The n=355 scaled replication is the strongest single piece of evidence in the paper, but it covers only Qwen3-32B x formality. The claim \"d converges to 0\" is established for this one cell. Verbosity shows d~0.15-0.19 at n=86, which could be either a small true effect attenuated by Likert or genuine measurement attenuation at a larger true effect. Without scaled replication for verbosity, the paper cannot definitively classify this cell. The bootstrap attenuation analysis (Appendix J) partially addresses this by showing d remains flat, but this is constrained by the original sample size.\n\n### Controls\n\n**1. Placebo control (double-rewrite).** Same-style double-rewrite shows judges prefer LLM-polished text (BT P(orig) = 0.08-0.42 across axes). This is an important finding that confirms a rewriting artifact exists. The paper argues GEE absorbs this artifact via the originality OR. This is mechanistically plausible: the originality predictor captures \"is original in preferred position,\" and the rewriting artifact would inflate this term. However, for verbosity, the originality OR is NS (0.49, p=0.096 for Qwen3), which means the placebo artifact (BT P(orig) = 0.08, p<0.0001 for Qwen3 verbosity) is *not* captured by the GEE model. This is a tension: a massive rewriting artifact exists (judges almost never prefer the original in verbosity), yet GEE says originality is non-significant. The resolution is that in the style-manipulation pairs (not the placebo pairs), the verbosity rewrite direction is bidirectional (verbose-to-concise and concise-to-verbose), and the style effect overwhelms the artifact. But this deserves more explicit discussion.\n\n**2. NLI gate ablation.** The reversal for formality NLI-fail pairs (BT 41.7%) is compelling evidence that the gate removes content contamination rather than amplifying signal. For verbosity, NLI-fail pairs remain at 75.0%, consistent with the dominant style effect. The formality reversal, however, is based on ~12 pairs, limiting precision.\n\n**3. Cross-axis gradient.** Three data points (register 55% < formality 67% < verbosity 77%) are presented as evidence against uniform format amplification. With only three points, this is qualitative at best. The paper appropriately labels this as a \"bounding analysis,\" but the cross-axis gradient is used more assertively in the anti-amplification argument (Section 4.4, item iii) than three data points warrant.\n\n**4. Claude negative control.** Claude Sonnet 4 showing no style bias (BT 56.2%/54.1%, NS) is useful discriminative evidence. One negative control is minimally sufficient; the ideal would be 2-3 judges showing no bias, or a judge known to be debiased. The TOST equivalence testing (Appendix E) bounds Claude's effect to <15pp, which is informative but not tight.\n\n**5. Rewriter independence.** GPT-4o as alternative rewriter produces statistically indistinguishable results from Qwen3-32B rewriter (formality: 64.4% vs. 66.5%; verbosity: 73.5% vs. 77.3%). This is a strong control for evaluator-producer bias, though the 22% decrease in Style OR for verbosity (13.10 to 10.17) suggests some evaluator-producer inflation. The paper acknowledges this.\n\n**Overall controls assessment.** The five-layer control structure is above-average for this literature. No single control is individually decisive, but the convergence of evidence is reasonably convincing that the detected effects are not pure artifacts. The weakest link is the format amplification argument: the Comparative Trap concern (Jeong et al. 2025) is real, and while the controls bound the risk, they do not eliminate it. The paper's dual-interpretation framing (measurement attenuation vs. construct divergence) is an honest way to handle this uncertainty.\n\n### Reproducibility\n\n**Experimental details.** The paper provides sufficient detail for reproduction: model identifiers (gpt-4o-2024-08-06), NLI model (DeBERTa-v3-large), temperature settings (0.7 for rewriting, 0.0 for judging), bootstrap parameters (10K resamples, seed 42), and NLI threshold (0.90). The rewriting prompts are detailed in the code but not fully reproduced in the paper; a supplementary appendix with exact prompts would help.\n\n**API-based judges.** GPT-4o and Claude Sonnet 4 are API models that may change behavior with version updates. The paper specifies the GPT-4o version (2024-08-06), which is good practice. Claude's version is identified as \"Claude Sonnet 4\" but without a specific snapshot date. Results may not replicate on future model versions.\n\n**Code and data.** \"Code and data will be released upon acceptance.\" This is standard but limits pre-publication verification. The codebase (from my inspection) is well-structured with separate modules for each pipeline stage.\n\n**Statistical software.** The paper uses the choix library for BT, statsmodels for GEE, and DeBERTa-v3-large for NLI. These are all publicly available. The BT regularization uses choix defaults, which should be stated explicitly (it uses a default regularization parameter that affects estimates when sample sizes are small).\n\n### Strengths (Methods/Experiments)\n\n1. **Comprehensive experimental matrix.** 4 judges x 3 benchmarks x 3 axes with dual measurement (Likert + pairwise) on identical data is a large and well-organized experimental design. The 12 tables covering distinct analyses show thoroughness.\n\n2. **Seven-method sensitivity hierarchy.** Testing the Blindspot with seven statistical methods (Table 2 and Appendix K) makes a convincing case that the finding is not an artifact of a particular statistical test. This is an unusually rigorous approach for the style-bias literature.\n\n3. **Honest epistemic framing.** The dual-interpretation approach (measurement attenuation vs. construct divergence) with the conclusion that both yield the same operational implication is scientifically mature. The Limitations section is substantive and identifies real weaknesses rather than token caveats.\n\n4. **GEE decomposition revealing per-axis confound structure.** The finding that verbosity is purely stylistic while formality is conditional and register is originality-dominated is a genuinely novel contribution that goes beyond simply detecting bias. The diagnostics (correlation structure comparison, interaction test) are thorough.\n\n5. **Direction-aware NLI gating.** The adaptation of the NLI gate for axes that inherently change information density (verbosity) is a methodological innovation that other researchers can adopt.\n\n### Weaknesses (Methods/Experiments)\n\n1. **[Moderate] Small primary dataset and limited scaled replication.** LLMBar has only 100 pairs, and the scaled replication (n=355) covers only one cell (Qwen3-32B x formality). The paper makes strong claims about a \"severity spectrum\" based on a handful of cells at different effect sizes, but these cells differ in benchmark, judge, and axis simultaneously, making it difficult to isolate what drives the spectrum. A controlled experiment varying only effect size (e.g., by titrating style intensity) would be needed to establish a true dose-response relationship.\n\n2. **[Moderate] The rewriting artifact threatens the pure-style claim for verbosity.** The placebo control shows verbosity has the most extreme rewriting artifact (Qwen3 BT P(orig) = 0.082), yet verbosity is presented as the \"cleanest\" axis with \"no observed originality confound.\" The GEE Originality OR being NS for verbosity may reflect the specific functional form of the model rather than the absence of artifact confounding. The paper's claim that \"the full 77.3% effect is attributable to style rather than originality\" is overstated given the placebo evidence. The convergent directional evidence (both directions favor verbose) is more convincing than the GEE NS result.\n\n3. **[Moderate] Multiple-comparison correction is incomplete across the full claim set.** The primary family (8 tests) is corrected, but the paper makes many additional claims: cross-benchmark replication, NLI ablation reversal, cross-axis gradient, debiased prompting effects, 100-point Likert NS, Claude equivalence. These are classified as \"exploratory\" or \"confirmatory\" with varying correction standards. While individual tests are valid, the cumulative probability of at least one false positive across the full analysis set is non-trivial.\n\n4. **[Minor] The \"90% Blindspot rate\" is fragile.** A denominator of 10 does not support a stable percentage. The three exceptions all involve stronger effects (BT > 69%), which actually undermines the Blindspot as a universal phenomenon and reframes it as a power-contingent effect. The paper acknowledges this (\"severity spectrum\") but the abstract and introduction lead with the 90% figure.\n\n5. **[Minor] No human evaluation of rewriting quality.** The NLI gate and LLM annotator validation are both automated. Human evaluation of a sample of rewritten texts would directly address the concern that NLI-passing pairs still differ in perceived quality. The Limitations section acknowledges this, but given how central content preservation is to the entire framework, even a small-scale human evaluation (50 pairs) would substantially strengthen the paper.\n\n### Missing Experiments\n\n1. **Scaled replication for verbosity.** The n=355 formality replication is the paper's strongest evidence, but verbosity shows d~0.15-0.19 at n=86 (borderline non-zero). A scaled verbosity replication would determine whether this represents genuine partial detection or measurement attenuation at a different point on the severity spectrum.\n\n2. **Human evaluation of rewriting quality.** A small-scale human study (e.g., 50 pairs x 2 annotators) rating content preservation would directly validate the NLI gate and address the most fundamental threat to validity.\n\n3. **Effect size titration.** The severity spectrum claim (complete Blindspot at moderate bias, partial at strong) would be more convincing with controlled variation of style intensity (e.g., parameterized rewriting prompts producing weak, moderate, and strong style shifts) on a single judge x benchmark.\n\n4. **Formal equivalence testing (TOST) for Likert.** The paper mentions this in Limitations but does not execute it for the main Blindspot cells. Demonstrating that the Likert effect is not just non-significant but formally equivalent to zero (TOST at a meaningful delta) would strengthen the measurement attenuation interpretation.\n\n5. **Position bias magnitude.** The GEE intercept captures position bias but its magnitude and significance are not reported. Given the extensive literature on position bias in LLM evaluation, this should be explicitly quantified.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. The placebo control shows massive rewriting artifacts for verbosity (P(orig) = 0.08), yet the GEE Originality OR for verbosity is NS. How do you reconcile these? Is it possible that the bidirectional design (verbose-to-concise + concise-to-verbose) masks the artifact rather than controls for it?\n\n2. The NLI gate uses direction-aware (unidirectional) verification for verbosity, which admits 17.4% false positives per GPT-4o validation. Have you assessed whether these false-positive pairs show systematically different BT estimates than true-positive pairs?\n\n3. For the GEE model: what is the estimated position bias (intercept term), and is it significant? Do results change if you add task-type fixed effects or random item intercepts?\n\n4. The 100-point Likert still shows 52-66% tie rates. Have you examined whether these ties reflect genuine indifference (the judge truly rates both texts identically) or a different mechanism (e.g., the judge defaulting to a round number)?\n\n5. The paper tests debiased prompting but only for the pairwise mode. What happens to the Likert Blindspot under debiased prompting --- does it narrow, widen, or remain unchanged?\n\n### Preliminary Scores\n\n- **Soundness:** 3.5/5. The pipeline is well-designed and the core finding (pairwise detects what Likert misses) is convincingly demonstrated. The GEE decomposition is a genuine contribution. Deductions for: incomplete reconciliation of placebo artifacts with pure-style claims, limited scaled replication, and the inherent difficulty of separating detection from amplification (though the controls help).\n\n- **Reproducibility:** 3.5/5. Sufficient detail for most analyses; specific model versions given for GPT-4o. Deductions for: code/data not yet released, exact rewriting prompts not in the paper, API model ephemerality, and choix library defaults not explicitly stated.\n\n- **Overall lean:** 3.5 (Borderline Conference). The paper addresses a timely and important problem (measurement methodology for LLM judge bias), introduces a well-engineered pipeline with genuine methodological innovations (direction-aware NLI gating, GEE per-axis decomposition), and the Likert Blindspot finding has clear practical implications. The experimental coverage is broad and the five-layer control structure is commendable. However, the primary dataset is small (n=100), scaled replication is limited to one cell, the format amplification concern is bounded but not eliminated, and the \"90% Blindspot rate\" overstates the strength of evidence from 10 cells. If the authors address the placebo-vs-GEE tension for verbosity and provide scaled replication for at least one additional axis, this would strengthen toward a 4.0. As submitted, the honest epistemic framing and methodological contributions place it above typical Findings-level work, but the residual concerns about whether pairwise is detecting or amplifying bias, combined with the limited dataset scale, keep it at the Conference borderline. Calibrating to EMNLP 2026 acceptance rates (~21% Main, Findings available at 3.0), this paper is competitive for Main track but would benefit from one round of targeted revision."}],"totalDurationMs":178419,"totalTokens":83677,"totalToolUseCount":20,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4022,"cache_read_input_tokens":74479,"output_tokens":5175,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4022},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":5175,"cache_read_input_tokens":74479,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4022,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4022,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":15,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":3}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"afa6b92585c3bea9a","tool_use_id":"toolu_01APExJ8cK9eGvZRntXSdyKN","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":88696,"tool_uses":23,"duration_ms":196675},"uuid":"e4fdd37b-d6f5-4284-8567-02a70c76f6e8","session_id":"84c1e33b-a907-4d62-a6bc-d61bef62f953"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01APExJ8cK9eGvZRntXSdyKN","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a comprehensive understanding of the paper, its experimental evidence, related work, and prior dream review feedback. Let me produce the R2 review focused on novelty, positioning, related work, and significance.\n\n## R2: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary of Assessment\nThis paper presents an empirically thorough diagnostic pipeline (CSD) for per-axis style bias in LLM judges and identifies the \"Likert Blindspot\" phenomenon. The per-axis confound decomposition via GEE is a genuinely novel analytical contribution. However, the headline finding that pairwise comparison is more sensitive than Likert scoring is a well-established result in psychometrics (Thurstone 1927) and evaluation methodology, making the core novelty primarily an empirical domain transfer rather than a conceptual advance. The paper has substantially improved its related work coverage and presentation since earlier drafts (now ~40 references, figures included), but still has positioning gaps against the closest concurrent competitors.\n\n### Novelty\n\n**C1 (CSD pipeline): Moderate novelty.** The combination of NLI-gated content control, per-axis style decomposition, and position-controlled pairwise comparison is a reasonable engineering contribution, but each component exists independently in prior work. NLI for content verification is standard in paraphrase evaluation. Style-controlled rewriting via LLM is used in RATE and Counterfactual Rhetorical. Position-controlled pairwise comparison is used in Chatbot Arena and AlpacaEval. The integration is competent but not surprising. The black-box applicability is genuinely useful, distinguishing CSD from FairJudge (requires training) and RATE (Likert-based). The claim of being \"the first framework combining\" these components is technically correct but represents systems-level integration novelty rather than conceptual novelty.\n\n**C2a (Likert Blindspot): Low-to-moderate novelty.** The core finding -- that pairwise comparison detects effects that absolute rating misses -- is predicted by Thurstone's Law of Comparative Judgment (1927) and has been empirically confirmed in NLG evaluation by Kiritchenko and Mohammad (2017), Novikova et al. (2018), and in survey methodology by Krosnick (1999). The paper commendably cites these and frames the Blindspot as \"the first systematic quantification of this gap for LLM judge style bias\" rather than claiming theoretical novelty. This is honest, but it means C2a is fundamentally a domain-transfer finding. The 100-point Likert ablation (ruling out granularity) and the bootstrap attenuation curve (distinguishing attenuation from power) are the strongest original evidence within C2a. The agnostic framing (attenuation vs. construct divergence) is appropriate and strengthens credibility. Still, a reviewer familiar with psychometrics will recognize this as confirming what measurement theory already predicts.\n\n**C2b (Per-axis confound structure): Highest novelty.** This is the paper's most genuinely novel contribution. The finding that different style axes have qualitatively different confound regimes -- verbosity is pure style (OR 8-13, Orig NS), formality is conditional (both sig), register is originality-dominated -- is not anticipated by prior work and has clear prescriptive value. No prior paper performs per-axis confound decomposition for LLM judge style bias. CASSE analyzes 12 style features but uses correlational methods without interventional controls. RATE applies interventional design but operates on a single aggregate axis. The three-regime taxonomy (pure/conditional/originality-dominated) is memorable and potentially useful as a framework for future work. The replication of confound structure across three independent architectures strengthens this claim considerably.\n\n**C3 (Architecture-dependent susceptibility): Low novelty.** The finding that 3/3 target judges show verbosity bias while Claude does not is useful validation but not surprising. Prior work (SOS-Bench, Soumik 2026, Preference Leakage) has already shown that style bias varies across judges. The Claude negative control is methodologically important (validates the pipeline does not fabricate false positives) but does not represent a novel finding per se.\n\n**GEE decomposition approach: Moderate novelty in context.** GEE (Liang and Zeger, 1986) is a standard biostatistics tool, not novel in itself. Its application to decompose style versus originality effects in LLM judge evaluation is new. The model is simple (two covariates, no interaction), but the paper shows the interaction term is non-significant, validating the additive specification. The correlation structure robustness analysis is thorough. However, the use of GEE for this specific decomposition is an application-level rather than methodological novelty.\n\n**Comparison to nearest competitors:**\n- **vs. RATE:** CSD uses pairwise BT instead of Likert ATE, which is the whole point of the Blindspot finding. CSD uses NLI gating instead of post-hoc correction. The papers are complementary: RATE estimates aggregate causal effects; CSD diagnoses per-axis style bias. The implicit critique of RATE (that its Likert-based measurements may miss effects) is well-supported by the evidence.\n- **vs. LC-AlpacaEval:** LC-AlpacaEval debiases at population level via regression on length. CSD operates at per-instance level across multiple style axes. CSD is diagnostic; LC-AlpacaEval is corrective. Different goals.\n- **vs. CASSE:** CASSE uses 12 style features with correlational analysis. CSD uses interventional design with NLI content control. CSD's interventional identification is a clear methodological advantage, but its scope (3 axes) is narrower than CASSE's (12 features).\n- **vs. Counterfactual Rhetorical:** Very similar pipeline concept (LLM rewrite + BT aggregation). CSD adds NLI gating, per-axis isolation, and GEE decomposition. The differentiation is real but the overlap is substantial and not sufficiently discussed in the paper.\n\n### Related Work\n\n**Coverage quality:** The paper has substantially expanded its related work section since earlier drafts. The current version cites approximately 40 references across four paragraphs. This is now within the acceptable range for EMNLP, though at the lower end. The organization into four paragraphs (Interventional Approaches, Measurement Sensitivity, Style Bias Detection, BT Models) is logical.\n\n**Adequacy of differentiation from key competitors:**\n- **Preference Leakage (Li et al., ICLR 2026):** Cited and discussed in the introduction and related work. The paper notes that preference leakage propagates through the evaluation pipeline via style, motivating per-axis isolation. This is adequate.\n- **FairJudge (Wang et al., 2026):** Cited and briefly mentioned in related work as relying on \"correlational analyses without interventional controls.\" This characterization is fair -- FairJudge's SFT/DPO/GRPO pipeline is training-based, while CSD is diagnostic. However, the differentiation could be sharpened: FairJudge actually debiases, while CSD only detects. This asymmetry should be acknowledged.\n- **SOS-Bench (Murugadoss et al., ICLR 2025):** Cited as foundational evidence for the severity of style bias (R=0.999). Adequate.\n- **RATE (Reber et al., ICML 2025):** Well-discussed in both related work and analysis sections. The head-to-head comparison (Section 5.2) is appropriate and fair. The framing of CSD as complementary to RATE is honest. The implicit critique (RATE may underestimate via Likert) is supported by evidence.\n- **Soumik et al. (2026):** Cited in the introduction (bias scores 0.76-0.92). Could benefit from more discussion of their finding that CoT is the only universally safe debiasing strategy, which is relevant to CSD's debiased prompting results.\n- **Counterfactual Rhetorical (Qiu et al., 2025):** Cited and differentiated on granularity (persona-level vs. axis-level). The differentiation is adequate but could note the shared BT aggregation approach more explicitly.\n\n**Missing or underdiscussed citations:**\n- **Park et al. (2024, ACL):** Cited as showing verbosity inflates scores, but their specific contribution on disentangling length effects is not adequately discussed given CSD's length confound analysis.\n- **Panickssery et al. (2024, NeurIPS) on self-preference:** Cited in Limitations but should be discussed in related work given the evaluator-producer bias issue with Qwen3-32B.\n- **DeCoster et al. (2011):** Cited for variance loss from categorization -- good, directly supports the Blindspot mechanism.\n\n**Is the positioning fair?** Mostly yes. The paper does not overclaim theoretical novelty for the Blindspot. The \"first systematic quantification\" framing is accurate. The dual-contribution positioning (Blindspot + per-axis confound structure) is strategically appropriate, as C2b carries more genuine novelty than C2a. The paper honestly acknowledges measurement theory precedents. The main positioning weakness is that CSD is framed as \"the first framework combining [these four components]\" -- this type of \"first to combine X, Y, and Z\" novelty claim is inherently weaker than \"first to discover [phenomenon].\"\n\n### Significance and Impact\n\n**Likert Blindspot finding:** Moderately impactful. The finding that Likert-only audits miss style bias at moderate effect sizes (BT 63-67%) is practically important for the evaluation community. The 100-point Likert experiment is the strongest evidence that this is a paradigm issue, not a granularity issue. The bootstrap attenuation curve at n=355 is convincing for the specific conditions tested (formality x Qwen3-32B). However, the practical impact is limited by scope: (a) most practitioners already use pairwise comparison for high-stakes evaluation (Chatbot Arena, AlpacaEval), so the recommendation to \"use pairwise\" is not surprising; (b) the Blindspot is complete only at moderate effects (d < 0.15) -- at stronger effects, Likert does detect bias, just attenuated.\n\n**Per-axis confound decomposition:** More impactful than the Blindspot for research methodology. The finding that verbosity is \"clean\" (directly interpretable) while formality requires decomposition and register claims are unsupported after decomposition is genuinely actionable. Future work measuring style bias can use this taxonomy to determine appropriate statistical methodology. The prescriptive value -- \"do not treat all style axes equally\" -- is a useful heuristic.\n\n**Would practitioners change pipelines?** Partially. The paper's recommendation (content-controlled pairwise comparison + per-axis decomposition for style bias audits) is sound but labor-intensive. Most practitioners will not adopt the full CSD pipeline for routine evaluation. However, the finding that Likert audits may produce false negatives could influence evaluation methodology choices in high-stakes settings (model selection, benchmark design). The absence of a correction/debiasing component limits immediate practical utility compared to FairJudge or LC-AlpacaEval.\n\n### Framing and Claims\n\n**\"90% blindspot rate\" (from the task description):** The paper does not actually use this framing in the text, which is appropriate. The evidence shows 8 of 11 bias-positive cells exhibit the Blindspot (Table 1), with the three exceptions being stronger effects. This is more nuanced than \"90%\" and the paper presents it correctly by noting the severity spectrum.\n\n**\"Five independent controls\" / \"six independent controls\":** The paper alternates between five and six controls across different sections (abstract mentions \"seven statistical methods,\" Section 5.2 lists five/six controls). These controls are not truly independent -- they share the same underlying data, rewriter, and NLI gate. A hostile reviewer could argue that if the NLI gate systematically biases the sample, all downstream analyses inherit that bias. The NLI threshold sweep (44/44 significant across 0.50-0.98) partially addresses this, but \"independent\" is overclaimed. More accurate: \"six complementary robustness checks.\"\n\n**\"First framework combining...\":** Technically accurate but weak as a novelty claim. The components are well-known; the integration is the contribution.\n\n**Scope acknowledgment:** The paper acknowledges key limitations: 3 axes, 4 judges, one primary benchmark (LLMBar n=100), Qwen3-32B as both rewriter and judge. The Limitations section is well-written and covers the right points (quality-style confounding, evaluator-producer bias, validation methodology). The paper appropriately treats register results as exploratory and acknowledges the scaled replication covers only one condition.\n\n**Sample size concerns:** n=88 (after NLI filtering from 100) is small for the claims being made. The BT confidence intervals are wide (e.g., GPT-4o formality: [0.557, 0.703]). The paper addresses this partly through the n=355 scaled replication for one condition, but the other 7 Blindspot cells rest on n~88. The power curve (Figure 4) is helpful but shows power at *observed* effect sizes, not a pre-specified minimum detectable effect.\n\n### Writing Quality\n\n**Organization:** Good. The four-section structure (Method, Experiments, Analysis, Conclusion) with a substantial Analysis section works well. The separation of main results (Table 1) from detailed decomposition analyses is logical.\n\n**Abstract:** Dense but accurate. It correctly summarizes both contributions and the agnostic framing. The sentence structure is complex; a simpler version would be more accessible.\n\n**Figures:** The paper now includes 4 figures (pipeline diagram, DAG, forest plot, power curve). These are functional but the pipeline diagram (Figure 1) is small and hard to read in the PDF. The forest plot (Figure 3) effectively communicates the main result. The power curve (Figure 4) supports the central argument. A visualization of the GEE confound structure (e.g., a grouped bar chart of Style OR vs. Orig OR across axes and judges) would be valuable and is absent.\n\n**Tables:** 14 tables across main text and appendix. The main text tables (1, 2, 3) are essential and well-designed. Table 1 is the key results table and is easy to parse with the dagger/double-dagger notation for Blindspot/both-sig. The appendix tables are thorough. However, some tables could be consolidated (e.g., Tables 6 and 7 in the appendix cover rewriter independence for verbosity and formality separately -- a single table would suffice).\n\n**Page count:** The main text (through Conclusion) occupies approximately 8 pages in the compiled PDF, with Limitations on page 9. Under strict EMNLP rules, Limitations and ethical considerations do not count toward the 8-page limit \"provided they do not contain excessive additional content.\" The current Limitations section is approximately 0.7 pages -- this is within the spirit of the rule. However, the main text is tight at exactly 8 pages, and a reviewer counting carefully might flag that some analysis in Section 5.6 could be considered results rather than analysis. This is borderline but defensible.\n\n**Writing style:** Clear, precise, appropriate for a technical audience. The paper avoids overclaiming and uses hedging language appropriately (\"interventional rather than formal causal evidence,\" \"provides filtering-based confounder control\"). The measurement theory framing (Thurstone, Stevens) adds credibility. One weakness: the paper is dense and could benefit from a \"key takeaways\" paragraph at the end of Section 5.\n\n### Strengths (Novelty/Significance)\n1. **Per-axis confound taxonomy is genuinely novel.** The three-regime classification (pure/conditional/originality-dominated) with consistent replication across three architectures is not anticipated by prior work and has clear prescriptive value for the evaluation community.\n2. **100-point Likert ablation is a clean experiment.** This is the strongest single piece of evidence for the Blindspot, ruling out the \"scale too coarse\" alternative and demonstrating a paradigm difference. Combined with the bootstrap attenuation curve, it makes a compelling case.\n3. **Honest and well-calibrated framing.** The agnostic stance on mechanism (attenuation vs. construct divergence), the acknowledgment of rewriting artifacts, and the treatment of register as exploratory demonstrate intellectual maturity. The paper does not overclaim.\n4. **Comprehensive related work with fair differentiation.** The paper cites and positions against all major competitors (RATE, CASSE, FairJudge, LC-AlpacaEval, Preference Leakage, Soumik, Counterfactual Rhetorical). The head-to-head comparison with RATE in Section 5.2 is particularly well-done.\n5. **Practical significance for evaluation methodology.** The finding that Likert-based null results may be false negatives due to measurement insensitivity rather than genuine absence of bias has implications for interpreting prior and future evaluation studies.\n\n### Weaknesses (Novelty/Significance)\n1. **[Major] Core finding is well-established in psychometrics.** The Likert Blindspot is a domain transfer of Thurstone (1927) and empirical findings from Kiritchenko and Mohammad (2017), Novikova et al. (2018). The paper's novelty is primarily in quantifying the gap for LLM judges specifically, not in discovering the gap. This limits the excitement ceiling.\n2. **[Major] Diagnostic-only contribution with no correction/mitigation.** CSD detects bias but offers no debiasing mechanism. The debiased prompting results (Section 5.6) show only partial reduction and are not presented as a contribution. Compared to FairJudge (which debiases) and LC-AlpacaEval (which corrects), CSD's practical utility is limited to auditing. A practitioner who learns their judge is biased still needs a separate solution.\n3. **[Moderate] Narrow empirical scope relative to claims.** Three style axes (two primary, one exploratory), one primary benchmark (LLMBar n=100), and one rewriter (Qwen3-32B for most conditions) limit generalizability. The \"three benchmarks\" framing is somewhat misleading as AlpacaEval and MT-Bench are tested only for formality. The claim that the Blindspot is \"structural\" rests heavily on the single scaled condition (n=355, formality x Qwen3-32B).\n4. **[Moderate] Overlap with Counterfactual Rhetorical not sufficiently differentiated.** The Qiu et al. (2025) pipeline (LLM rewrite + BT aggregation) is very similar to CSD's core design. The differentiation (persona-level vs. axis-level, sign test vs. BT) exists but is narrow. A reviewer who knows this paper well might question whether CSD's incremental additions (NLI gating, GEE decomposition) constitute sufficient novelty over this baseline.\n5. **[Minor] \"Five/six independent controls\" overclaimed.** The controls share underlying data and pipeline components. They are complementary robustness checks, not independent lines of evidence. The NLI ablation reversal is genuinely informative; the others provide weaker evidence.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n1. How does CSD compare to simply running the Counterfactual Rhetorical pipeline (Qiu et al., 2025) with axis-specific rather than persona-level prompts? What is the marginal contribution of NLI gating and GEE decomposition beyond their framework?\n2. The bootstrap attenuation curve is shown only for formality x Qwen3-32B. Can you show equivalent curves for verbosity and for GPT-4o/Llama-70B to demonstrate that the \"d converges to zero\" pattern is not condition-specific?\n3. You show debiased prompting partially reduces verbosity bias (-13.1pp) but barely affects formality (-0.4pp for GPT-4o). Does this asymmetry hold for Llama-70B? And does the GEE confound structure predict which axes are prompt-correctable?\n4. The NLI gate uses DeBERTa-v3-large at threshold 0.90. Given that verbosity's direction-aware gating has 17.4% false positives (GPT-4o validation), how confident are you that the verbosity \"pure style\" classification is not an artifact of more permissive content filtering?\n5. The paper presents the Blindspot as a problem for evaluation. But given that Jeong et al. (2025) show pairwise comparison amplifies bias, isn't it possible that the \"true\" bias level is between the Likert estimate (near zero) and the BT estimate (63-77%)? How would you arbitrate this without human ground truth?\n6. Your Limitations note that \"whether findings transfer to settings with longer outputs, domain-specific evaluation, or human judges requires further investigation.\" Do you have any preliminary evidence or theoretical reason to expect the Blindspot transfers to human judges, given that the psychometric literature already shows pairwise > Likert for humans?\n\n### Preliminary Scores\n- Excitement: 3.0/5\n- Overall lean: 3.5 (Borderline Conference)\n\n**Justification:** The per-axis confound structure (C2b) is the paper's most genuinely novel contribution and is well-executed. The Likert Blindspot (C2a), while practically important, is fundamentally a domain transfer of established psychometric findings, limiting excitement. The three-architecture replication and five-layer defense are unusually thorough for this area. The paper is well-written with honest framing. However, the diagnostic-only scope (no debiasing), narrow empirical coverage (one primary benchmark, two primary axes), and overlap with Counterfactual Rhetorical limit the impact. At EMNLP 2026 calibration, this is a strong Findings paper. To reach 4.0 (Main Conference), the paper would need either (a) a debiasing contribution building on the diagnostic findings, (b) substantially broader empirical coverage demonstrating generalization, or (c) theoretical analysis of when and why the Blindspot occurs that goes beyond the empirical observation. The current version is competent, thorough, and honest, but the novelty ceiling imposed by psychometric precedent keeps it below the Main track threshold."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: afa6b92585c3bea9a (use SendMessage with to: 'afa6b92585c3bea9a' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 93385\ntool_uses: 23\nduration_ms: 196676</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"84c1e33b-a907-4d62-a6bc-d61bef62f953","uuid":"c15703f0-1cf2-412e-9af2-830a65e0c925","timestamp":"2026-05-21T16:15:25.362Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus: **Novelty, positioning, related work, and significance**.\n\n## Venue Calibration (EMNLP 2026 ARR)\n- 5 = Award consideration (top 2.5%)\n- 4 = Conference-worthy (Main track, ~21% acceptance)\n- 3 = Findings-worthy\n- 2 = Major revision needed\n- 1 = Do not resubmit\nDimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5)\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition\"\n\nThe paper introduces Controlled Style Decomposition (CSD), a diagnostic pipeline for per-axis style bias in LLM judges. Key novelty claims:\n1. **Likert Blindspot (C2a)**: First systematic comparison of Likert vs pairwise measurement on identical stimuli for LLM judge style bias. Finding: pairwise detects style bias (BT 63–77%) that Likert misses (d≈0) in 9/10 cells — 90% blindspot rate.\n2. **Per-axis confound structure (C2b)**: GEE decomposition reveals three qualitatively distinct regimes — pure style (verbosity), conditional (formality), originality-dominated (register). No prior work characterizes different confound structures across style axes.\n3. **CSD pipeline (C1)**: Reusable black-box diagnostic combining NLI-gated content control, per-axis manipulation, position-controlled comparison, and GEE decomposition.\n4. **Architecture-dependent susceptibility (C3)**: Verbosity bias replicates across 3/3 architectures; formality is 2/3 (Llama-70B doesn't show formality bias).\n\n**Related work cited** (16 references):\n- Zheng et al. 2023 (MT-Bench/Chatbot Arena)\n- Li et al. 2023 (AlpacaEval)\n- Dubois et al. 2024a (AlpacaFarm), 2024b (LC-AlpacaEval)\n- Chiang et al. 2024 (Chatbot Arena)\n- Ouyang et al. 2022 (InstructGPT)\n- Rafailov et al. 2023 (DPO)\n- Wu and Aji 2023 (Style over substance, SOS-Bench R=0.999)\n- Park et al. 2024 (Disentangling length, ACL)\n- Stureborg et al. 2024 (LLM bias taxonomy)\n- Chen et al. 2024 (Humans or LLMs as judge, EMNLP)\n- Saito et al. 2023 (Verbosity bias)\n- Jeong et al. 2025 (Comparative trap — pairwise amplification)\n- Lu et al. 2024 (CASSE — multi-dimensional analysis)\n- Thurstone 1927 (Comparative judgment)\n- Stevens 1946 (Measurement scales)\n- Zeng et al. 2024 (LLMBar)\n\n**Key competitors/foundations from the research landscape** (for your reference):\n- **Preference Leakage (ICLR'26)**: Model family relationships leak preferences through style; contextual calibration reduces Error Bias by 59%\n- **Soumik (2026)**: CoT is only universally safe debiasing strategy; style bias 0.76–0.92 >> position bias ≤0.04\n- **FairJudge (2026)**: Training-side debiasing (SFT→DPO→GRPO), 8B beats 72B/671B\n- **LC-AlpacaEval (ICLR'25)**: Length as causal mediator, regression debiasing\n- **SOS-Bench (ICLR'25)**: Style-overall R=0.999\n- **RATE (ICML'25)**: Double-rewrite ATE for counterfactual measurement\n- **CASSE (2026)**: Style feature causal entanglement, mitigation has alignment tax\n- **Counterfactual Rhetorical**: Persona counterfactual + BT for rhetorical style\n\n## Your Task\nProduce a detailed review addressing:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**\n   - How novel is each of the four contributions (C1-C3, C2a, C2b)?\n   - Is the Likert Blindspot finding genuinely new, or is the Likert-vs-pairwise sensitivity difference well-known in psychometrics?\n   - Is the GEE decomposition approach novel in this context?\n   - How does CSD compare to RATE's double-rewrite approach, LC-AlpacaEval's regression, and CASSE's multi-dimensional analysis?\n\n2. **Related Work Coverage**\n   - Are major competitors adequately cited and differentiated?\n   - Missing citations? Consider: Preference Leakage (ICLR'26), FairJudge (2026), SOS-Bench (ICLR'25), RATE (ICML'25), Soumik (2026)\n   - Is the positioning fair to prior work? Does the paper overstate its novelty?\n\n3. **Significance and Impact**\n   - How impactful is the Likert Blindspot finding for the NLP evaluation community?\n   - Does the per-axis confound decomposition provide actionable insights?\n   - Is the finding that \"verbosity is pure style but formality is conditional\" genuinely useful?\n   - Would practitioners change their evaluation pipelines based on this work?\n\n4. **Framing and Claims**\n   - Are the claims well-calibrated to the evidence?\n   - The \"90% blindspot rate\" — is this framing appropriate given only 10 cells?\n   - \"Five independent controls\" — are they truly independent?\n   - Does the paper adequately acknowledge limitations of its scope (3 style axes, 4 judges)?\n\n5. **Writing Quality**\n   - Is the paper well-organized?\n   - Is the abstract accurate and informative?\n   - Are the 12 tables well-motivated and easy to parse?\n   - Is the paper within 8-page limit for main text? (It appears to be ~9 pages of main text before Limitations/References)\n\n6. **Excitement Assessment**\n   - What would make this paper more exciting?\n   - Is it incremental or does it open new research directions?\n\nOutput format:\n```\n## R2: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary of Assessment\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Novelty\n[detailed assessment of each contribution]\n\n### Related Work\n[coverage gaps, positioning fairness]\n\n### Significance and Impact\n[assessment]\n\n### Framing and Claims\n[calibration assessment]\n\n### Writing Quality\n[assessment]\n\n### Strengths (Novelty/Significance)\n1. ...\n2. ...\n3. ...\n\n### Weaknesses (Novelty/Significance)\n1. [severity] ...\n2. [severity] ...\n3. ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\n1. ...\n\n### Preliminary Scores\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Overall lean: X.X (with brief justification calibrated to EMNLP 2026 acceptance rates)\n```\n","agentId":"afa6b92585c3bea9a","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a comprehensive understanding of the paper, its experimental evidence, related work, and prior dream review feedback. Let me produce the R2 review focused on novelty, positioning, related work, and significance.\n\n## R2: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary of Assessment\nThis paper presents an empirically thorough diagnostic pipeline (CSD) for per-axis style bias in LLM judges and identifies the \"Likert Blindspot\" phenomenon. The per-axis confound decomposition via GEE is a genuinely novel analytical contribution. However, the headline finding that pairwise comparison is more sensitive than Likert scoring is a well-established result in psychometrics (Thurstone 1927) and evaluation methodology, making the core novelty primarily an empirical domain transfer rather than a conceptual advance. The paper has substantially improved its related work coverage and presentation since earlier drafts (now ~40 references, figures included), but still has positioning gaps against the closest concurrent competitors.\n\n### Novelty\n\n**C1 (CSD pipeline): Moderate novelty.** The combination of NLI-gated content control, per-axis style decomposition, and position-controlled pairwise comparison is a reasonable engineering contribution, but each component exists independently in prior work. NLI for content verification is standard in paraphrase evaluation. Style-controlled rewriting via LLM is used in RATE and Counterfactual Rhetorical. Position-controlled pairwise comparison is used in Chatbot Arena and AlpacaEval. The integration is competent but not surprising. The black-box applicability is genuinely useful, distinguishing CSD from FairJudge (requires training) and RATE (Likert-based). The claim of being \"the first framework combining\" these components is technically correct but represents systems-level integration novelty rather than conceptual novelty.\n\n**C2a (Likert Blindspot): Low-to-moderate novelty.** The core finding -- that pairwise comparison detects effects that absolute rating misses -- is predicted by Thurstone's Law of Comparative Judgment (1927) and has been empirically confirmed in NLG evaluation by Kiritchenko and Mohammad (2017), Novikova et al. (2018), and in survey methodology by Krosnick (1999). The paper commendably cites these and frames the Blindspot as \"the first systematic quantification of this gap for LLM judge style bias\" rather than claiming theoretical novelty. This is honest, but it means C2a is fundamentally a domain-transfer finding. The 100-point Likert ablation (ruling out granularity) and the bootstrap attenuation curve (distinguishing attenuation from power) are the strongest original evidence within C2a. The agnostic framing (attenuation vs. construct divergence) is appropriate and strengthens credibility. Still, a reviewer familiar with psychometrics will recognize this as confirming what measurement theory already predicts.\n\n**C2b (Per-axis confound structure): Highest novelty.** This is the paper's most genuinely novel contribution. The finding that different style axes have qualitatively different confound regimes -- verbosity is pure style (OR 8-13, Orig NS), formality is conditional (both sig), register is originality-dominated -- is not anticipated by prior work and has clear prescriptive value. No prior paper performs per-axis confound decomposition for LLM judge style bias. CASSE analyzes 12 style features but uses correlational methods without interventional controls. RATE applies interventional design but operates on a single aggregate axis. The three-regime taxonomy (pure/conditional/originality-dominated) is memorable and potentially useful as a framework for future work. The replication of confound structure across three independent architectures strengthens this claim considerably.\n\n**C3 (Architecture-dependent susceptibility): Low novelty.** The finding that 3/3 target judges show verbosity bias while Claude does not is useful validation but not surprising. Prior work (SOS-Bench, Soumik 2026, Preference Leakage) has already shown that style bias varies across judges. The Claude negative control is methodologically important (validates the pipeline does not fabricate false positives) but does not represent a novel finding per se.\n\n**GEE decomposition approach: Moderate novelty in context.** GEE (Liang and Zeger, 1986) is a standard biostatistics tool, not novel in itself. Its application to decompose style versus originality effects in LLM judge evaluation is new. The model is simple (two covariates, no interaction), but the paper shows the interaction term is non-significant, validating the additive specification. The correlation structure robustness analysis is thorough. However, the use of GEE for this specific decomposition is an application-level rather than methodological novelty.\n\n**Comparison to nearest competitors:**\n- **vs. RATE:** CSD uses pairwise BT instead of Likert ATE, which is the whole point of the Blindspot finding. CSD uses NLI gating instead of post-hoc correction. The papers are complementary: RATE estimates aggregate causal effects; CSD diagnoses per-axis style bias. The implicit critique of RATE (that its Likert-based measurements may miss effects) is well-supported by the evidence.\n- **vs. LC-AlpacaEval:** LC-AlpacaEval debiases at population level via regression on length. CSD operates at per-instance level across multiple style axes. CSD is diagnostic; LC-AlpacaEval is corrective. Different goals.\n- **vs. CASSE:** CASSE uses 12 style features with correlational analysis. CSD uses interventional design with NLI content control. CSD's interventional identification is a clear methodological advantage, but its scope (3 axes) is narrower than CASSE's (12 features).\n- **vs. Counterfactual Rhetorical:** Very similar pipeline concept (LLM rewrite + BT aggregation). CSD adds NLI gating, per-axis isolation, and GEE decomposition. The differentiation is real but the overlap is substantial and not sufficiently discussed in the paper.\n\n### Related Work\n\n**Coverage quality:** The paper has substantially expanded its related work section since earlier drafts. The current version cites approximately 40 references across four paragraphs. This is now within the acceptable range for EMNLP, though at the lower end. The organization into four paragraphs (Interventional Approaches, Measurement Sensitivity, Style Bias Detection, BT Models) is logical.\n\n**Adequacy of differentiation from key competitors:**\n- **Preference Leakage (Li et al., ICLR 2026):** Cited and discussed in the introduction and related work. The paper notes that preference leakage propagates through the evaluation pipeline via style, motivating per-axis isolation. This is adequate.\n- **FairJudge (Wang et al., 2026):** Cited and briefly mentioned in related work as relying on \"correlational analyses without interventional controls.\" This characterization is fair -- FairJudge's SFT/DPO/GRPO pipeline is training-based, while CSD is diagnostic. However, the differentiation could be sharpened: FairJudge actually debiases, while CSD only detects. This asymmetry should be acknowledged.\n- **SOS-Bench (Murugadoss et al., ICLR 2025):** Cited as foundational evidence for the severity of style bias (R=0.999). Adequate.\n- **RATE (Reber et al., ICML 2025):** Well-discussed in both related work and analysis sections. The head-to-head comparison (Section 5.2) is appropriate and fair. The framing of CSD as complementary to RATE is honest. The implicit critique (RATE may underestimate via Likert) is supported by evidence.\n- **Soumik et al. (2026):** Cited in the introduction (bias scores 0.76-0.92). Could benefit from more discussion of their finding that CoT is the only universally safe debiasing strategy, which is relevant to CSD's debiased prompting results.\n- **Counterfactual Rhetorical (Qiu et al., 2025):** Cited and differentiated on granularity (persona-level vs. axis-level). The differentiation is adequate but could note the shared BT aggregation approach more explicitly.\n\n**Missing or underdiscussed citations:**\n- **Park et al. (2024, ACL):** Cited as showing verbosity inflates scores, but their specific contribution on disentangling length effects is not adequately discussed given CSD's length confound analysis.\n- **Panickssery et al. (2024, NeurIPS) on self-preference:** Cited in Limitations but should be discussed in related work given the evaluator-producer bias issue with Qwen3-32B.\n- **DeCoster et al. (2011):** Cited for variance loss from categorization -- good, directly supports the Blindspot mechanism.\n\n**Is the positioning fair?** Mostly yes. The paper does not overclaim theoretical novelty for the Blindspot. The \"first systematic quantification\" framing is accurate. The dual-contribution positioning (Blindspot + per-axis confound structure) is strategically appropriate, as C2b carries more genuine novelty than C2a. The paper honestly acknowledges measurement theory precedents. The main positioning weakness is that CSD is framed as \"the first framework combining [these four components]\" -- this type of \"first to combine X, Y, and Z\" novelty claim is inherently weaker than \"first to discover [phenomenon].\"\n\n### Significance and Impact\n\n**Likert Blindspot finding:** Moderately impactful. The finding that Likert-only audits miss style bias at moderate effect sizes (BT 63-67%) is practically important for the evaluation community. The 100-point Likert experiment is the strongest evidence that this is a paradigm issue, not a granularity issue. The bootstrap attenuation curve at n=355 is convincing for the specific conditions tested (formality x Qwen3-32B). However, the practical impact is limited by scope: (a) most practitioners already use pairwise comparison for high-stakes evaluation (Chatbot Arena, AlpacaEval), so the recommendation to \"use pairwise\" is not surprising; (b) the Blindspot is complete only at moderate effects (d < 0.15) -- at stronger effects, Likert does detect bias, just attenuated.\n\n**Per-axis confound decomposition:** More impactful than the Blindspot for research methodology. The finding that verbosity is \"clean\" (directly interpretable) while formality requires decomposition and register claims are unsupported after decomposition is genuinely actionable. Future work measuring style bias can use this taxonomy to determine appropriate statistical methodology. The prescriptive value -- \"do not treat all style axes equally\" -- is a useful heuristic.\n\n**Would practitioners change pipelines?** Partially. The paper's recommendation (content-controlled pairwise comparison + per-axis decomposition for style bias audits) is sound but labor-intensive. Most practitioners will not adopt the full CSD pipeline for routine evaluation. However, the finding that Likert audits may produce false negatives could influence evaluation methodology choices in high-stakes settings (model selection, benchmark design). The absence of a correction/debiasing component limits immediate practical utility compared to FairJudge or LC-AlpacaEval.\n\n### Framing and Claims\n\n**\"90% blindspot rate\" (from the task description):** The paper does not actually use this framing in the text, which is appropriate. The evidence shows 8 of 11 bias-positive cells exhibit the Blindspot (Table 1), with the three exceptions being stronger effects. This is more nuanced than \"90%\" and the paper presents it correctly by noting the severity spectrum.\n\n**\"Five independent controls\" / \"six independent controls\":** The paper alternates between five and six controls across different sections (abstract mentions \"seven statistical methods,\" Section 5.2 lists five/six controls). These controls are not truly independent -- they share the same underlying data, rewriter, and NLI gate. A hostile reviewer could argue that if the NLI gate systematically biases the sample, all downstream analyses inherit that bias. The NLI threshold sweep (44/44 significant across 0.50-0.98) partially addresses this, but \"independent\" is overclaimed. More accurate: \"six complementary robustness checks.\"\n\n**\"First framework combining...\":** Technically accurate but weak as a novelty claim. The components are well-known; the integration is the contribution.\n\n**Scope acknowledgment:** The paper acknowledges key limitations: 3 axes, 4 judges, one primary benchmark (LLMBar n=100), Qwen3-32B as both rewriter and judge. The Limitations section is well-written and covers the right points (quality-style confounding, evaluator-producer bias, validation methodology). The paper appropriately treats register results as exploratory and acknowledges the scaled replication covers only one condition.\n\n**Sample size concerns:** n=88 (after NLI filtering from 100) is small for the claims being made. The BT confidence intervals are wide (e.g., GPT-4o formality: [0.557, 0.703]). The paper addresses this partly through the n=355 scaled replication for one condition, but the other 7 Blindspot cells rest on n~88. The power curve (Figure 4) is helpful but shows power at *observed* effect sizes, not a pre-specified minimum detectable effect.\n\n### Writing Quality\n\n**Organization:** Good. The four-section structure (Method, Experiments, Analysis, Conclusion) with a substantial Analysis section works well. The separation of main results (Table 1) from detailed decomposition analyses is logical.\n\n**Abstract:** Dense but accurate. It correctly summarizes both contributions and the agnostic framing. The sentence structure is complex; a simpler version would be more accessible.\n\n**Figures:** The paper now includes 4 figures (pipeline diagram, DAG, forest plot, power curve). These are functional but the pipeline diagram (Figure 1) is small and hard to read in the PDF. The forest plot (Figure 3) effectively communicates the main result. The power curve (Figure 4) supports the central argument. A visualization of the GEE confound structure (e.g., a grouped bar chart of Style OR vs. Orig OR across axes and judges) would be valuable and is absent.\n\n**Tables:** 14 tables across main text and appendix. The main text tables (1, 2, 3) are essential and well-designed. Table 1 is the key results table and is easy to parse with the dagger/double-dagger notation for Blindspot/both-sig. The appendix tables are thorough. However, some tables could be consolidated (e.g., Tables 6 and 7 in the appendix cover rewriter independence for verbosity and formality separately -- a single table would suffice).\n\n**Page count:** The main text (through Conclusion) occupies approximately 8 pages in the compiled PDF, with Limitations on page 9. Under strict EMNLP rules, Limitations and ethical considerations do not count toward the 8-page limit \"provided they do not contain excessive additional content.\" The current Limitations section is approximately 0.7 pages -- this is within the spirit of the rule. However, the main text is tight at exactly 8 pages, and a reviewer counting carefully might flag that some analysis in Section 5.6 could be considered results rather than analysis. This is borderline but defensible.\n\n**Writing style:** Clear, precise, appropriate for a technical audience. The paper avoids overclaiming and uses hedging language appropriately (\"interventional rather than formal causal evidence,\" \"provides filtering-based confounder control\"). The measurement theory framing (Thurstone, Stevens) adds credibility. One weakness: the paper is dense and could benefit from a \"key takeaways\" paragraph at the end of Section 5.\n\n### Strengths (Novelty/Significance)\n1. **Per-axis confound taxonomy is genuinely novel.** The three-regime classification (pure/conditional/originality-dominated) with consistent replication across three architectures is not anticipated by prior work and has clear prescriptive value for the evaluation community.\n2. **100-point Likert ablation is a clean experiment.** This is the strongest single piece of evidence for the Blindspot, ruling out the \"scale too coarse\" alternative and demonstrating a paradigm difference. Combined with the bootstrap attenuation curve, it makes a compelling case.\n3. **Honest and well-calibrated framing.** The agnostic stance on mechanism (attenuation vs. construct divergence), the acknowledgment of rewriting artifacts, and the treatment of register as exploratory demonstrate intellectual maturity. The paper does not overclaim.\n4. **Comprehensive related work with fair differentiation.** The paper cites and positions against all major competitors (RATE, CASSE, FairJudge, LC-AlpacaEval, Preference Leakage, Soumik, Counterfactual Rhetorical). The head-to-head comparison with RATE in Section 5.2 is particularly well-done.\n5. **Practical significance for evaluation methodology.** The finding that Likert-based null results may be false negatives due to measurement insensitivity rather than genuine absence of bias has implications for interpreting prior and future evaluation studies.\n\n### Weaknesses (Novelty/Significance)\n1. **[Major] Core finding is well-established in psychometrics.** The Likert Blindspot is a domain transfer of Thurstone (1927) and empirical findings from Kiritchenko and Mohammad (2017), Novikova et al. (2018). The paper's novelty is primarily in quantifying the gap for LLM judges specifically, not in discovering the gap. This limits the excitement ceiling.\n2. **[Major] Diagnostic-only contribution with no correction/mitigation.** CSD detects bias but offers no debiasing mechanism. The debiased prompting results (Section 5.6) show only partial reduction and are not presented as a contribution. Compared to FairJudge (which debiases) and LC-AlpacaEval (which corrects), CSD's practical utility is limited to auditing. A practitioner who learns their judge is biased still needs a separate solution.\n3. **[Moderate] Narrow empirical scope relative to claims.** Three style axes (two primary, one exploratory), one primary benchmark (LLMBar n=100), and one rewriter (Qwen3-32B for most conditions) limit generalizability. The \"three benchmarks\" framing is somewhat misleading as AlpacaEval and MT-Bench are tested only for formality. The claim that the Blindspot is \"structural\" rests heavily on the single scaled condition (n=355, formality x Qwen3-32B).\n4. **[Moderate] Overlap with Counterfactual Rhetorical not sufficiently differentiated.** The Qiu et al. (2025) pipeline (LLM rewrite + BT aggregation) is very similar to CSD's core design. The differentiation (persona-level vs. axis-level, sign test vs. BT) exists but is narrow. A reviewer who knows this paper well might question whether CSD's incremental additions (NLI gating, GEE decomposition) constitute sufficient novelty over this baseline.\n5. **[Minor] \"Five/six independent controls\" overclaimed.** The controls share underlying data and pipeline components. They are complementary robustness checks, not independent lines of evidence. The NLI ablation reversal is genuinely informative; the others provide weaker evidence.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n1. How does CSD compare to simply running the Counterfactual Rhetorical pipeline (Qiu et al., 2025) with axis-specific rather than persona-level prompts? What is the marginal contribution of NLI gating and GEE decomposition beyond their framework?\n2. The bootstrap attenuation curve is shown only for formality x Qwen3-32B. Can you show equivalent curves for verbosity and for GPT-4o/Llama-70B to demonstrate that the \"d converges to zero\" pattern is not condition-specific?\n3. You show debiased prompting partially reduces verbosity bias (-13.1pp) but barely affects formality (-0.4pp for GPT-4o). Does this asymmetry hold for Llama-70B? And does the GEE confound structure predict which axes are prompt-correctable?\n4. The NLI gate uses DeBERTa-v3-large at threshold 0.90. Given that verbosity's direction-aware gating has 17.4% false positives (GPT-4o validation), how confident are you that the verbosity \"pure style\" classification is not an artifact of more permissive content filtering?\n5. The paper presents the Blindspot as a problem for evaluation. But given that Jeong et al. (2025) show pairwise comparison amplifies bias, isn't it possible that the \"true\" bias level is between the Likert estimate (near zero) and the BT estimate (63-77%)? How would you arbitrate this without human ground truth?\n6. Your Limitations note that \"whether findings transfer to settings with longer outputs, domain-specific evaluation, or human judges requires further investigation.\" Do you have any preliminary evidence or theoretical reason to expect the Blindspot transfers to human judges, given that the psychometric literature already shows pairwise > Likert for humans?\n\n### Preliminary Scores\n- Excitement: 3.0/5\n- Overall lean: 3.5 (Borderline Conference)\n\n**Justification:** The per-axis confound structure (C2b) is the paper's most genuinely novel contribution and is well-executed. The Likert Blindspot (C2a), while practically important, is fundamentally a domain transfer of established psychometric findings, limiting excitement. The three-architecture replication and five-layer defense are unusually thorough for this area. The paper is well-written with honest framing. However, the diagnostic-only scope (no debiasing), narrow empirical coverage (one primary benchmark, two primary axes), and overlap with Counterfactual Rhetorical limit the impact. At EMNLP 2026 calibration, this is a strong Findings paper. To reach 4.0 (Main Conference), the paper would need either (a) a debiasing contribution building on the diagnostic findings, (b) substantially broader empirical coverage demonstrating generalization, or (c) theoretical analysis of when and why the Blindspot occurs that goes beyond the empirical observation. The current version is competent, thorough, and honest, but the novelty ceiling imposed by psychometric precedent keeps it below the Main track threshold."}],"totalDurationMs":196676,"totalTokens":93385,"totalToolUseCount":23,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4715,"cache_read_input_tokens":83371,"output_tokens":5298,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4715},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":5298,"cache_read_input_tokens":83371,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4715,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4715,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":13,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":7}}}
